{"timestamp": 1778125127.668139, "duration_sec": 3.9712867736816406, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "have bought G2.1 not too long ago. So we sort of positioned ourselves in the groovy grades area rather than the Scala area. But I mean, still, if there is any project around, I would certainly love to hear about it. Yeah, so would I. So guys, listeners, you know how to reach us, team at seradio.net. And I'll forward it to you."], [0, "Great. Last question. Spring is an open source project. However, there is this big powerful company behind it. So how many of the Spring developers are non-Spring source or how does this work? I think recently there was this conflict kind of thing where Spring source made certain, I think, bug fixes people had to pay for or something. So what's the open source story here?"], [0, "Well, the open source story is that Spring is an open source project. It will always stay that way and we won't change the policy, the license. That's also what our CEO Rod Johnson said in his blog. I would argue that if you think about it"], [0, "One of the hurdles that open source has to take for usage in companies is the question, where do you get support? If there are just some people who develop an open source project and there is no one who can actually stand up and say, okay, well, we are going to give you this kind of service level agreement. We are going to support you in that way. It's probably not going to be easy to use it in a professional environment."], [0, "This is not related to the quality of the open source project or anything. It's not about whether this support will actually be needed. You need somebody to blame if you're a big company. That's what it boils down to exactly. And I mean, that's also why if you look at commercial software, why most of the time you have some kind of support contract."], [0, "There were some miscommunications, in particular miscommunications, about the maintenance policy that's already, it's more or less a year ago. And we actually changed it as well. So let me give you an idea about what the current policy says."], [0, "If you look at open source again at other open source projects, it's quite common for the projects to sort of go for the next version and invest all the resources in the next version. And I think that's pretty, I mean, it's completely understandable and it makes a lot of sense because you want to innovate and you want to do the new cool features and so on."], [0, "However, what this means in a professional environment is that you better go for each and every new version. And you cannot, I would argue, you cannot afford that. In some cases, I mean, operations people will just say, well, this is not an option at all. We have to stick to the old version. So what we have done now is we have a policy in place that says each version of Spring will be supported for three years."], [0, "each major version there will be binary releases for that major version until the next major version comes out so if spring 3.0 comes out we will continue to provide binary versions for spring 3.0 as would every any other open source project as well and"], [0, "However, the add-on that we have because of the commercial support is that you can also stick to 2.5 and you can get binaries from us if you have support contract. In other words, you have people pay you for doing the boring work of keeping the old stuff alive."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["open source", "spring framework", "software licensing", "commercial support", "maintenance policy", "enterprise software", "service level agreement", "bug fixes", "software development", "versioning", "long term support", "release management", "software contracts", "professional environment", "operations teams"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778126114.0338292, "duration_sec": 6.6381447315216064, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "August 27, 2025\nA regular meeting of the City Council of the City of Boston was held in the Christopher A. Iannella\nChamber, City Hall on Wednesday, August 27, 2025 at 12:08 P.M.\nPresident Louijeune in the Chair. Present Councilors Breadon, Durkan, FitzGerald, Flynn, Mejia,\nMurphy, Pep\u00e9n, Santana, Weber, and Worrell. Absent Councilor Coletta Zapata.\nFranklin Hobbs, delivered the invocation and the meeting was opened with the pledge of allegiance.\nThe Chair moved to adopt the minutes from the August 6, 2025 meeting.\nThe Chair moved to adopt the amended minutes from the December 11, 2024 meeting.\nMotions prevailed.\n_________________________________________________________________________\n1548 Message and order authorizing the City of Boston to accept and expend the\namount of Four Million One Hundred Twenty-Five Thousand Dollars\n($4,125,000.00) in the form of a grant, for the FY26 Public Safety\nAnswering Point Support and Incentive Grant, awarded by MA Executive\nOffice of Public Safety & "]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["city council of the city of boston", "city of boston", "boston city hall", "christopher a. iannella chamber", "president louijeune", "councilor breadon", "councilor durkan", "councilor fitzgerald", "councilor flynn", "councilor mejia", "councilor murphy", "councilor pep\u00e9n", "councilor santana", "councilor weber", "councilor worrell", "councilor coletta zapata", "message and order 1548", "fy26 public safety answering point support and incentive grant", "$4", "125", "000.00", "massachusetts executive office of public safety"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778126498.0394242, "duration_sec": 2.047330617904663, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "It accomplishes this other thing of kind of protecting like the intrinsic motivation of the people in your company doing the work. I actually find a pretty strong like emotional component to it, which I don't know if you intended or if that's just like a happy byproduct. But when you talk explicitly about when you're."], [0, "Thinking about your appetite, you're thinking, how do I want to feel at the end of those six weeks? Even the idea of something like a circuit breaker, which seems kind of harsh, it actually still is about protecting how people feel because you don't want people to feel like garbage at the end of six weeks of work."], [0, "Absolutely. Yeah, absolutely. You know, so much of these problems that we frame as functional problems, you know, the thing didn't get shipped, we didn't hit the deadline, like the tool, the feature doesn't do what it's supposed to do and we never fix that bug. These functional things are issues, but the real issue is always how it makes us feel. So, for example, here's so many stories from people where they're a product manager and"], [0, "they're responsible for trying to take the product to a better place strategically. You know, they're supposed to have some vision for where the product's supposed to go, but they don't have any time to think about where the product is supposed to go because they're in meetings all the time. And then on top of it, people are coming to them with questions like, how should this be? How should that be? And so they end up facing the question of,"], [0, "On the weekend, it's like, do I spend time with my kid or do I do the work that I didn't get to do during the week? You know what I mean? Or like when my friend invites me out for a drink on a Thursday night and it's like, I can't do that because I have to work at night because I didn't get to work during the day because it's the only chance I'm ever going to get to actually think. Yeah. You know? So much of the book is actually about that. If you shape up a project \u2013"], [0, "That's at the right level of abstraction. So it's not so concrete that you've done all the work for the team. And it's not so fuzzy that they don't know what it is. But you use the techniques that we show in the book to shape the project at the right level of roughness. That it shows them the boundaries of kind of here are the main components. Here's how it's going to work. And here's why we believe in it. And they can see the solution at this very sketchy level. Yeah."], [0, "If you have that, if you've done that pre-work, you can give that to a team and say, here's the rough solution. There's a million details for you to work out within that. I'm going to give you six weeks to do that. And if it doesn't happen, there's no second chances. So take it seriously. And at the same time, I promise to never interrupt you. Yeah. Yeah."], [0, "What an amazing thing, right? Because then what happens is the team gets empowered. They have the skills to make all the implementation decisions, whether it's design or coding. And by giving the teams, you give them the shaped project, but you don't give them any tasks."], [0, "You just give them a really, really clear goal and you say, you guys create the tasks. You all figure out together what you need to do to make this happen. And then they're figuring out what the work is. And because of that..."], [0, "They are not done when they check off the box that somebody gave them. They're done when the thing makes sense. And then while the team is autonomously building out this thing for a whole six weeks, you don't have to manage them. That's the beautiful thing about you empower them and then that frees you, right? To do more shaping. Exactly. So now all of a sudden you actually have the room for that shaping track. So you have the whole six weeks to think strategically. Right."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["product management", "project planning", "software development", "team autonomy", "intrinsic motivation", "employee wellbeing", "workplace culture", "time management", "deadlines", "strategic thinking", "leadership", "agile methods", "task prioritization", "design process", "productivity"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778126599.081568, "duration_sec": 2.081914186477661, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "It goes beyond the plot, right? You can for sure return a plot in the XKCD theme, but you could also have the whole demo in the XKCD theme. I often pull this example up, this theme, this XKCD thing for Matplotlib, because it's fun. But also, I think there's genuine value in putting together something that looks like this. Because if you show this to decision makers, bosses, managers, types, and they look at something that looks like it's working, and they're like, oh,"], [0, "Well, we're done then. No, we have two months more work. We're not done. But I click the button and it's giving me answers. We're really not done. It's not scalable. It's not this. It's not that, right? It's only an estimate. Just an XKCD front end on it. You're like, look, you see it's not done. It's just, it's got squiggly lines. It's hand-drawn. It's clearly a prototype. Like, oh yeah, okay. But I can see where this is going. I think actually, psychologically, it may have a big impact, even though it's silly."], [0, "Yeah, it's a super interesting point. I never thought about it that way. But yeah, I mean, I think it definitely gives it a little bit more sketch vibe. Like this is like in the... Yeah, like a wireframe vibe. Yeah. Yeah, wireframe, like straight from the workshop. Exactly. Yeah, that's what I was thinking because I presented projects to various stakeholders when I used to do that kind of stuff. And they'd be like, oh, well, that looks like it's done. No, we're going to need some time because it's really not done."], [0, "I know it looks good, but it's not. Yeah, yeah, yeah. You made it look too good, basically. Yeah, exactly. That was a serious mistake. Yeah. OK, so we got a little bit more time to talk about a couple of things. I want to talk about how people actually share this. We're still talking about a thing I pip install locally, and it has a UI, but what do I do?"], [0, "I still don't want to set up a Linux machine and Nginx and domains and all that. So what are the options? But before we get to that, tell us a bit about the internals. Like when you guys work on Gradio and my pip install it, like what's running? What is this project? The backend is a fast API server. So what Gradio will do, it'll spin up a server for you. And then that server will serve like the front end assets. The front end is built in Svelte. Interesting. Okay. Yeah."], [0, "Basically, whenever these reactivity events happen, what will happen is that the front end will just call the backend API and then run your function and then make sure that all the necessary processing that needs to happen to get your data ready happens. But at the end of the day, it's a simple model in that sense. Obviously, there's some more complications with the streaming, for example. That's a whole different code path almost. But at the end of the day, it's like a REST server that's"], [0, "talking with a javascript client so it's like the standard developer tools story for python people is it's not some of it is python but you probably end up writing a lot of javascript or typescript to build this tool for other people right so they don't have to i'm not a huge svelte expert like thankfully there's some of the people i work with they're like really good at they're really knowledgeable and that stuff and yeah like the front-end code i think it's i think there's more svelte code than python code actually i'm curious like what the"], [0, "What's the code breakdown? Let's break it down. Yeah. 65% Python, 16% Svelte, 13% TypeScript. Well, so I think the reason might be that we have a lot of like demos and stuff. Yeah. I think there might be some stuff like that. Yeah. The demos are in there. Yeah. There's a lot of demos. You know what feature GitHub needs as you navigate the source tree, right? When I click on like client or demo or Gradio, it would be awesome if those stats would also be repeated, but just for that section of code, wouldn't that be great? Like how much of the demos are Python?"], [0, "I don't know. Maybe I just want to know that, but that'd be cool. Anyway, yeah. So I suspect that is, there's probably a lot of code and you've got a lot of notebooks and stuff in there too. That probably puts a big change on it there. A lot of the code is actually, yeah, JavaScript is felt. Right. It's the take one for the team. So the rest of us don't have to write JavaScript. Exactly. Yeah. Cool. Interesting. Very nice. And it says it can be embedded in notebook, which is interesting, or it can be presented as a webpage. Tell us about this part."], [0, "If you were to run this on any notebook, like Google Colab, for example, I think this might be an example, right? So if you call like the way that Gradio works, right? Once you create your Gradio interface or blocks, once you call launch, that's how you start up the server. That's like you kick off the whole process of serving this. That'll create the server locally, right? So no data is like leaving your machine, right? And then if you click, if you call launch on like a Jupyter notebook, Colab, SageMaker, like the UI will display like in the notebook."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["python", "gradio", "web apps", "user interface", "prototyping", "visualization", "matplotlib", "xkcd style", "stakeholder communication", "frontend development", "backend development", "api server", "fastapi", "svelte", "jupyter notebooks"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
