{"timestamp": 1778128780.2539134, "duration_sec": 2.4653403759002686, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "For example, even the programming language that you use is going to change the way you use your hands on a day-to-day basis. Now this is a seemingly insignificant change, of course,"], [0, "If you're writing Lisp, for example, then you're going to be much more able to find the parentheses keys, right? Because there's significantly more parentheses designed into the tool that is Lisp. But if you're a developer and you're like most developers, then you're also going to very likely spend a lot of your day sitting at a desk or standing at a desk staring at a screen, right?"], [0, "And in this way, our tool is shaping us, quite literally shaping our physical bodies. And in what other ways can our tools shape us? Well, even from a cultural perspective, let's take Ruby for example. One of the main kind of ideas in the Ruby community is to optimize for developer happiness."], [0, "What does this mean? Well, it's a value set that the developers of Ruby have continued to pursue over the years that Ruby has been around, and it informs the way that the language is designed, but it also informs the way that the community engages with the language."], [0, "To various degrees, Ruby developers will view Ruby from the perspective or maybe from the kind of evaluation point of how happy does Ruby actually make me. So even in the intention of the tool, we are shaping later behavior."], [0, "So why does this matter? What is so important about this? Well, the takeaway that I want you to have from this episode is that as you choose your tools, I want you to think more about how consequential those choices may be."], [0, "Now, we're not talking about how consequential they are in terms of your kind of marketability as a developer. Of course, that's something that is relevant and something that you should consider, but it's not the only consequence that you have as a result of learning or using a particular tool."], [0, "I talked further about this with Mark Engelberg, the creator of the code game series. These are board games for children and adults and everything in between that teach you programming concepts. Mark and I discussed learning first through a functional paradigm."], [0, "So learning through, in particular, Mark is a proponent of closure and learning closure first and how learning closure first can change the way you think about programming. Because this tool is designed in a particular way. We have a shape with our tools."], [0, "Our tools have a form, some way of existing and behaving. And because we use them, because we interface with those tools, we also take a shape. We also conform in some way to that tool. So what we should do or what we have the opportunity to do is try to determine the form that we want to take from."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["programming languages", "software development", "lisp", "ruby", "functional programming", "tool choice", "developer culture", "developer happiness", "learning programming", "clojure", "programming education", "ergonomics", "productivity", "human behavior", "technology influence"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
