{"timestamp": 1778136986.7871234, "duration_sec": 2.2314088344573975, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "this kind of closes our episode would you mind summing up the what we just learned as a closing sure"], [0, "Design by contract is a way of thinking about interfaces, about abstract types, and thinking about them not only in terms of operations and their signatures, but also in terms of the semantics behind those operations. That is, it's not only the name of the operation and the names and types of the parameters and the return type, but also under what circumstances does this operation do what,"], [0, "And what do I as a caller have to do for this operation to do what it promises to do? This is the key thing about design by contract and if that's the only thing you remember from this episode, that's great. But you get additional benefit if you have tool support for this. There are ways of writing formalized conditions."], [0, "as annotations to the operations depending on the tool, depending on the programming language. And then these are checked at runtime. And this is obviously additional benefit. And these tools also ensure that contracts are maintained in inheritance hierarchies. They ensure that every subtype offers at least as good a contract as the supertype does."], [0, "So these are additional benefits you get if you use tools, but even if you just keep this mindset of an operation is more than just a signature, it is a contract, it is a list of guarantees that I as a caller have to provide, and then the object guarantees to do certain other things,"], [0, "And this is not part of the implementation, but it is part of the interface. This way of thinking about interfaces, about interface operations in terms of contracts is really valuable, even if you don't use tools. Great. Thank you, Arno, for explaining the sign-by-contract to us. Thank you, Michael. And thanks, everybody, for listening. Hear you next week. Bye-bye. Bye."], [0, "thanks for listening to software engineering radio if you want to get more information about software engineering radio or if you want to give us feedback please go to our website at se-radio.net you can also contact the team at team at se-radio.net although we prefer entries in our comment system on the website so other people can see what you think"], [0, "Software Engineering Radio wants to thank Henning Pauli for the intro and outro music, as well as Libsyn for providing the bandwidth. This episode of SE Radio, as well as all other episodes, is licensed under a Creative Commons license. See the Software Engineering Radio website for details."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["design by contract", "software engineering", "interfaces", "abstract data types", "api design", "programming languages", "formal specification", "preconditions", "postconditions", "runtime checking", "tool support", "inheritance", "subtyping", "type safety", "software correctness"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778137944.6031754, "duration_sec": 2.246782064437866, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "it is harder to predict because we can't really calculate how often the server is up because it might go down tomorrow for 10 days and then everything is different again. So reliability is a little bit in the middle. So what do you consider to be reliability? And please give us another juicy anecdote where stuff went wrong because we love to hear that."], [0, "Yeah, I agree with your assessment. It's somewhat in between because basically you can only measure reliability after the fact. But that's kind of too late. You'd like to measure it beforehand. And once again, you have some proxies here, some proxy measures that you can take beforehand. For example, testing coverage is the measure that comes to mind."], [0, "That's a proxy that you can measure before deployment, that it's a rough indication of reliability. It's one of the few things you can measure that's related to reliability, at least. So in the book, I take two different points of view. So I have two chapters about reliability. One is called Reliability Through Monitoring."], [0, "And by that, I basically mean what's commonly known as defensive programming, or rather an expanded form of defensive programming. Because usually by defensive programming, you mean checking preconditions of methods at runtime, basically. That's the basic version of defensive programming."], [0, "But that's only a part of a larger framework that's called the design by contract, a more general idea. And within this idea, you also have invariants, class invariants, and you have post conditions."], [0, "So if you're really serious about reliability, and you want to achieve that through monitoring, so through online checking that everything is going fine, then you may want to also check invariance while the program is running, and you may also want to check post conditions."], [0, "while the program is running. So every method checks that it's called with the right parameters. That's its precondition. Then it checks that the current object is in the right state before it starts. That's checking the invariant. And then after the method finishes its job,"], [0, "It also checks that the method itself has done its job properly, which sounds kind of crazy because you're writing both the method and the procedure that checks that the method is correct. But sometimes it can be useful, but it's very expensive in terms of..."], [0, "both time and space, most of the time, if you want to add all these checks. So this kind of extreme defensive programming is reserved for special contexts where, let's say, safety-critical"], [0, "applications. It can be software for an industrial plant, software for the automotive industry, health industry, those applications where a bug could be catastrophic, could lead to loss of life or loss of huge amounts of money, huge damage. And in this respect, since I'm talking about safety critical software, let me also mention that you can, speaking of"]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["software reliability", "server uptime", "monitoring", "defensive programming", "design by contract", "testing coverage", "preconditions", "postconditions", "invariants", "software testing", "safety critical systems", "automotive software", "industrial control", "healthcare software"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
