{"timestamp": 1778141620.461922, "duration_sec": 1.4174683094024658, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah, I was just thinking that that would be a very scary introduction to Ruby, I think. No context and just kind of like, oh yeah, you can do this, but then you've got to follow it up with people don't do this all the time. It's not the first minute. We show other things first. I show normal stuff first. Got it."], [0, "But I do make a point of showing that bit. First of all, because it's a fun demo. And second of all, because I think you need to know what the boundaries are and that they're different than what you might be used to. I mean, we start with, this is what a string literal looks like. You start with, this is what a string literal looks like. And step two is, and here's how we can use metaprogramming to make it do something you would never imagine. No, that's like step 10 or 11. It's hard because it's not a very long onboarding session."], [0, "And we do have people who come through it who are relatively new to Ruby. So the goal is just to give a sense of what people about Ruby love about Ruby, where you can go for more information, and to just sort of give a grounding in it rather than try to explore every little corner of language because you have a limited amount of time. And it's meant to be. It's onboarding. It's not the end of the person's experience. So you want them to have a good first experience. You spend a lot of time teaching people Introduction to Ruby."], [0, "I'm curious for yourself if you learned anything new about Ruby as part of writing the book. I did learn a lot of stuff. And I learned a lot of stuff from the existing text of the book, which was kind of embarrassing. I learned some things about... Well, first of all, to write the book, I learned a lot about the new features of Ruby. I learned a ton about pattern matching and about Ractors and things like that because those needed to have multi-page big sections in the book. So I needed to really understand how those worked. And there were three or four things like that that I really needed to..."], [0, "learned that I hadn't had an opportunity to learn, but there were important enough parts of Ruby that they had to have significant, especially pattern matching. I really needed to have some space, and I hadn't used it very much. But there are also corners of the regular expression universe in Ruby that are extremely powerful that I didn't really know about. I learned about a lot of different parts of the Ruby standard library that I hadn't used before that were cool."], [0, "And that I hadn't really explored very much. I definitely learned things. The act of writing the book was tremendously fun. I really enjoyed it. It was a fun thing to sit down to and get to do. Everything about it was just really fun. I don't know if you have really talked about this, but do you dabble in any other languages these days? Yeah."], [0, "not as much right now because my job is very specifically ruby focused but i do write javascript and front-end stuff as needed so you know i still write rails apps that have some front-end components so i still work in javascript a little bit i haven't really played a whole lot with other programming languages recently like i've gone through phases like i've done python in the past and i've worked with"], [0, "I went through an Elm phase for a while where I tried to do a bunch of stuff in Elm. I've used a couple of different JavaScript frameworks and I've used TypeScript and things like that. But yeah, so I do have some experience and I historically have used a lot of different languages in the past. I have a lot of different kinds of angles to come at things with, but my day-to-day right now is largely weird. Do you have anything, Noel, to say about the reception to it or just now it's been in the world for a few months now?"], [0, "Want to go in that direction? As I said, I get less feedback than you would probably expect, but it has been very nice. People have been very warm about it, just about the fact that it exists, that it is back in the world. People at conferences and places where I have been out in the community, people have come to me to express that they're very happy that the book is out. I think the most consistent feedback that I've gotten is just people that are happy that it's being continued. But if people want to leave Amazon reviews, I'm not going to stop them."], [0, "We love a good review. Yeah, good reviews. I mean, good reviews are good. Other than Amazon, where people can leave you a five-star review, where else can they find the Pickaxe book or your work in general? So the Pickaxe book is available at Amazon or Bookshop or wherever technical books are sold. You can also get it in e-book from the Pragmatic site. It's pragprog.org. And you can find it there. You can find my blog at noelrapin.com."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["ruby", "programming", "metaprogramming", "onboarding", "teaching", "technical books", "software development", "pattern matching", "concurrency", "standard library", "regular expressions", "web development", "javascript", "rails", "programming languages"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778142140.809287, "duration_sec": 3.9878766536712646, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "That's all for me too. I mean, I'm just as passionate about, you know, Aerosmith as I am about the Isley Brothers. But I've never looked at life as I have to think in this parameter. You know, I've got to be marginalized. That's just\u2014 man, come on. That's such a waste of life."], [1, "It is."], [0, "It's all for you, man. So\u2014 I agree. With the martial arts and everything else, I look at every martial art. Just like everything else, everything has something to contribute. Just like all people have something to contribute. Even an idiot, you can learn from an idiot."], [1, "You can, you can. A lot of idiots say wise things occasionally."], [0, "Yeah, because everybody's going to have a quotient of legitimacy. Maybe it may be 20% and they don't see the 80%, but until you acknowledge that 20%, they're not gonna hear you. You know what I mean? So that's the thing. Man, we're on this planet, and one of the things I meant, I don't envy a whole lot of people, but dude, I do envy you because you get to expand your world. You talk to so many interesting people, and that's, what a great thing. What a great thing to just have all these type of perspectives and all that coming through. And I gotta say, man, I'm super proud of you 'cause I know you as Joe from the gym, and look what you've done, man. Man, it's like that's a shot in the arm because it's like people that you like and seeing them prosper, that's cool as shit."], [1, "Yeah, I've learned a lot, man. And I didn't expect to, you know, when we first started doing this, it was just for fun. We just get together with our friends, you know."], [0, "But you knew what you wanted to do, man. You were pretty damn clear because do you remember this? You remember me coming to, I think it was the Ice House?"], [1, "In Pasadena?"], [0, "Yeah. No, no, no. Oh shoot. It wasn't Ice House. It was in It was in Orange County. I came to see\u2014 Comedy Magic Club, maybe? I came to see you perform, and I offered you the role in Blood and Bone. Do you remember that at all?"], [1, "I do! Yeah, yeah, I do now. Yeah, okay."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["music", "rock", "soul", "aerosmith", "martial arts", "personal growth", "learning", "wisdom", "open mindedness", "diversity", "perspectives", "friendship", "comedy", "acting", "career success"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778142144.853472, "duration_sec": 1.4705169200897217, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Dude."], [1, "Or did you have like the first, was it the first rumbles?"], [0, "I thought it was, I thought I reacted to the, like some kind of an aftershock or some kind of rumble. No, because the girl that was with."], [1, "Me\u2014 you left her in the apartment."], [0, "Dude. All she knows is that you jumped up and you ran out of\u2014 you, you ran out of the house, and I heard the, the, uh, the door slide, and that was\u2014 and then the next thing you know, everything shook. She couldn't\u2014 she was trapped in there. Because there was a, a closet door that trapped, um, trapped her in the hallway. So when I got back in the place, me and a friend had to try to pry the door open because she couldn't get out. But I ran out of that place before the earthquake actually happened."], [1, "How weird. Yeah, you got good instincts."], [0, "I don't know what the hell that was."], [1, "It has to be because I don't."], [0, "Even like telling that story because it sounds like bullshit. It really happened that way because then the guard\u2014 I talked to the guard. I'm like, hey, when did the lights go out? Oh, it shook and the lights went out. I'm like, I'm watching that happen."], [1, "So you felt it happen before it happened."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["earthquake", "aftershock", "tremor", "natural disaster", "emergency", "evacuation", "instinct", "fear", "safety", "building", "power outage", "rescue", "trapped", "shock"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778142212.612072, "duration_sec": 1.7576005458831787, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "But here's what our brain didn't really predict, what our level of evolution couldn't predict. And that is that we encounter people who are in our out groups far more regularly now than we did even 50 years ago. Certainly much more regularly than we did a thousand years ago."], [0, "And so our preconceived notions about what a given person is like are very often going to be wrong. They're going to be limited. And for that reason, we may make judgments ahead of time about what our kind of relational dynamics should be like with this person."], [0, "So what do we do about this? Well, there's not really any prescriptive way to combat bias necessarily. By understanding that this exists, by understanding that you view outgroups as having much less diversity than your in-groups do, it makes sense for you to build relationships with those people and teach your brain about the diversity in those groups."], [0, "Before a meeting with someone who is a part of your out group, remind yourself that this person is an individual that are in and out groups. These social constructs that we have don't necessarily define the way that an individual will be."], [0, "This is very important for managers. It's very important for people who are in hiring roles. It's important for people who are in sales roles even. This becomes important the more and more kind of exposure that you have to mixing groups, people who are not necessarily in your day-to-day in-group."], [0, "the more exposure you have outside of those end groups, the more likely it is that your assumptions about those people can come back to haunt you and make your relationships, at the very least, less than optimal."], [0, "Unfortunately, we can't really eliminate biases selectively. We can't choose to not have them. This is actually something called, this is a bonus one, the bias bias. The idea that if you're simply aware of a bias that you can somehow escape the effects of it. And this is simply not true. So instead, we need to come up with alternative ways, alternative routes for our behaviors, right? And finding ways to counteract."], [0, "these biases, and sometimes ways to simply appreciate them. Thank you so much for listening to today's episode of Developer Tea. Thank you again to Linode for sponsoring today's episode. Head over to linode.com slash developer tea. Also, by the way, Linode is hiring. Head over to linode.com slash careers to check out the open positions."], [0, "Thank you so much for listening to today's episode. Today's episode was produced by Sarah Jackson. My name is Jonathan Cottrell. And until next time, enjoy your tea."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["cognitive bias", "social psychology", "in group", "out group", 