tality Net\nAs seen in the Q1 2026 U.S. Hotel Construction Pipeline Trend Report from Lodging Econometrics (LE), at the end of the first quarter, there are 6,020 projects with 705,825 rooms in the pipeline , reflecting sustained development activity across the country."], [3, "CBRE Group - Wikipedia\nFor investors, CBRE provide capital markets (property sales, commercial mortgage brokerage, loan origination and servicing), property leasing, investment management, property management, sustainability, valuation and development services, among others.[8]."], [4, "Q1 report : U.S. hotel pipeline hits 6,020 projects - Asian Hospitality\nReport : U.S. pipeline hits 6,020 projects in Q1. 126 new hotels opened, adding 14,614 rooms. The U.S. pipeline first quarter report , according to Lodging Econometrics."]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["seattle city council", "public safety committee", "seattle municipal code chapter 14.125", "council bill 121164", "ordinance relating to civil immigration enforcement", "councilmember robert kettle", "councilmember rob saka", "councilmember debora juarez", "councilmember eddie lin", "councilmember maritza rivera", "councilmember dionne foster", "councilmember alexis mercedes rinck", "seattle city hall", "seattle", "explore seattle southside", "puget sound business journal", "visit seattle", "lodging econometrics", "q1 2026 u.s. hotel construction pipeline trend report", "cbre group", "united states"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778144659.284884, "duration_sec": 1.9191687107086182, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And I find it so inspiring because metaphors are such a powerful way of looking at something. Metaphors have a way of explaining nuances that is very difficult to do without metaphors. I'm going to use a metaphor to show you just how powerful metaphors can be."], [0, "When I think about the world, I think about it through the lens of development. And this allows me to see different connections between things that otherwise I would not have seen as easily. If I only thought about development when I was coding instead of having a lens of development when I'm looking at the world, then I wouldn't be as good of a developer because code is actually a representation of a reality that we create in a computer."], [0, "So now I've used the metaphor of a lens to explain to you how I look at the world and I apply concepts of development to the world. Now the reason this metaphor is so powerful is because all of us are relatively familiar with"], [0, "with what a lens does. A lens changes our perception of whatever it is that we are looking at. And when I say that I use development as a lens to look at the world, you now understand that I am seeing the world differently, not as a literal lens over my eyes physically, but instead as a way of perceiving the world differently through the perspective of development."], [0, "We can see very quickly that metaphors help us communicate an underlying truth that is more difficult to communicate with more words, but that's not the only thing metaphors are good for. In fact, metaphors can be very helpful in the problem-solving process. If we can look at one problem and help explain it using a metaphor, then we can uncover nuances of the original problem that otherwise may be elusive."], [0, "And the reason for this is because we can look at the metaphor and then differentiate our original problem from the nuances of the metaphor that we already understand. Now I've been using the word metaphor, but in reality we use metaphors and similes and they have basically the same function as I'm referring to it in this discussion. A metaphor is when you actually say that something is something else. For example, time is money."], [0, "As similarly as when you say something is like something else, such as time is like money. I'm going to take a quick sponsor break and then I'm going to give you one tip to creating useful and meaningful metaphors in your day-to-day work. You've probably heard of QuickBooks and you might even use QuickBooks every day in your small business. But did you know that QuickBooks has an API that"], [0, "Intuit has built the API with developers in mind using standards like OpenID, OAuth, and REST API calls. And with millions of businesses already using QuickBooks, you've got a customer base that's ready to use your app. And you can even publish your app on apps.com."], [0, "Intuit's application marketplace built specifically for QuickBooks users. And here's the best part of the QuickBooks API and apps.com. It's all free. Intuit doesn't take a royalty share from the applications that you publish. You can get up and running in just a few minutes using the Developer Sandbox and the API Explorer. Just go to developer.intuit.com today to get started. There will also be a link in the show notes to let Intuit know that you're a Developer Tea listener, which is a huge help to the show."], [0, "Check it out in the show notes on developer t.com."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["metaphor", "simile", "communication", "language", "storytelling", "explanation", "problem solving", "perspective", "cognition", "software development", "programming", "coding", "application programming interface", "web services", "authentication"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778144701.6490276, "duration_sec": 2.3898136615753174, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[3, "This is your town. This is your station."], [0, "This is Waitely."], [7, "Good morning!"], [3, "Righto, who's riding the hopper meter today? If ever there was a time to feel the optimism, it's now. We sampled that with Carlton last week. From those I've encountered across the weekend, it had spread to the Bulldogs and St Kilda. A Saints fan told me this morning the needle had spiked through the red and crashed out the right-hand side of the gauge. St Kilda will run favourites on Sunday night, won't they? Is that the way that it's all heading? It seeped into Melbourne, where a WhatsApp group on Friday night upgraded the Dees to wildcard contenders. Lachie Doveston gave North a little burst of joy late yesterday in what had been a pretty tense afternoon, I feel. Perhaps the only team that crashed off the hope-o-meter was West Coast, and hope is a foreign concept for the Eagles in recent times. There's all manner of curiosity from the Community Series matches, and we're canny enough not to get too carried away, but above all, you want it to be the source of hope, to ground some optimism. So as we hit our snap judgements, Who's riding the hopper meter? 1300 736 736 is the Radius Telematics open line. Fleet managers and business owners, take control, visit radius.com. 1300 736 736. And the Forty Wings temper text is 0433 9811 16. The difference is temper. Snap judgments is what you feel most passionately from the weekend of sports. The most beautiful moment was Alyssa Healy's century in her final ODI for Australia. Afflicted by cramps, The retiring champ took the long handle to India in an innings to showcase her brilliance. The most timely happening was Sam Kerr scoring the first goal of the Asian Cup amid widespread recognition Matilda fever isn't what it used to be and broad debate as to why. Nothing would revive the spark more than winning. This is a team that hasn't won terribly much in recent times, and winning through the deeds of Sam Kerr and Mary Fowler that will grab the front pages in the coming weeks. The saddest news was the passing of Makybe Diva, prompting an emotional wave of remembrance. The greatest hero from our greatest race. Phar Lap ran in 3 Melbourne Cups and won 1. Makybe won all 3 that she contested. That question regarding the Melbourne Cup tends to answer itself. She's part of a trio of modern mares who achieved extraordinary feats. Black Caviar's unbeaten career winks with her 4 Cox Plates and litany of records, but Mokaibi Diva's 3 Melbourne Cups is just so improbable. A handicap event that should in theory guard against it happening, yet she did it in the most unforgettable of circumstances, and she was remembered with great affection over the past 24 hours. Las Vegas was suitably flashy for the NRL. More than 45,000 fans to greet the start of the NRL season. It feels big. Code wars aside, I continue to admire the ambition. Vegas has it all over opening rounds, and Peter Velandis is declaring victory this morning."], [7, "Look at it, definitely the biggest. There's no doubt about that. Crowd-wise, and certainly from people coming from Australia, it's by far the biggest attendance we've had of over 20,000 Australians, 10,000 from England. And but the good thing is there's 5,000 from other parts of the world. So Anyone that says we're not a global game hasn't been to Vegas."], [8, "Andrew Liveris, the head of the 2032 Olympics, and a story today now emerging he wants the NRL and the AFL to play a part in the Olympics."], [7, "I don't know about the FLA, but he certainly wants the NRL. Yeah, look, he was very impressed with the way things are organised here. He's certainly learning a lot with, you know, the Brisbane Olympics. He's done a lot of work here himself in LA with the LA Olympics, and, you know, naturally we want to be part of the Olympics and promote our game. And, you know, we might look at putting a State of Origin match on in the middle of it."], [3, "The AFL, I imagine we're supposed to feel offended, but it does tend to make me laugh. The boss of the Brisbane Olympics wants AFL and NRL to really show off what we are as a country during the 2032 Games. So I'll ask Robert Craddock about this with all of his wisdom and experience. There is, of course, precedent. The VFA beat a combined VFL team at the MCG in 1956. I think it was ahead of the bronze medal match for soccer. So there might be a future. Know your history. In that as to what's going to happen for the Brisbane Olympics. It is all fertile ground for snap judgments. 1300 736 736. Sport is our safe space in troubling times this morning, and we are not insulated from events in the Middle East. A critical mass of the Formula One world has been in Bahrain for testing in the lead-up to this week's Australian Grand Prix, but there is optimism from Travis Old that this week's event won't be disrupted."], [8, "The events of the weekend have certainly meant there's been some reshuffling of some travel plans, but the Formula One organisation are very good at moving people around the world. That's what they do. And so they've been able to do that and we're not expecting any impacts on our race. A lot of this is done by Formula One. So you're talking about teams, drivers, Formula One personnel. I'm guessing there'd be close to 1,000 people that would have already booked their flights and would be landing somewhere between sort of today, tomorrow, Wednesday. So they had to all be changed. But a lot of people around the world are doing the same thing. And so you're competing obviously with that, that increase in demand. So but they've been able to sort it out is the important part. All the freight is sort of here and ready to go. And so we're in a space where we're really confident there'll be no impact."], [3, "The cars and the drivers are here. So the Australian Grand Prix hoping to be unaffected. The most peculiar scenario belongs to the Asian Cup. So Australia is drawn to play Iran on Thursday night on the Gold Coast. At this moment in time, it's hard to imagine the Iran national anthem playing while an Australian team stands across in front of a near full house. I'd imagine there's a fair degree of diplomacy to play out from here. So much for the wisdom and experience of Robert Craddock. The Monday Means Test returns. Adam Simpson is working away, but get your snap judgments in before we let Simmo loose. Connor's in Castlemaine. Hello to you, Connor."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Sports"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["sports", "australian football", "rugby league", "cricket", "soccer", "horse racing", "formula one", "olympics", "las vegas", "australian grand prix", "season preview", "fan optimism", "international events", "middle east", "sports media"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778145103.8961346, "duration_sec": 2.2004334926605225, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah, who wrote Watership Town? I have no idea. All right. So my book of this show is Words of Radiance, which is part of the Stormlight Archive series by Brandon Sanderson. And I recommended, this is the second book, and I recommended the first one previously, I'm quite sure, The Way of Kings. And so I recently finished the second book. And this is, I guess..."], [0, "on schedule for once because the third book is coming out uh november 14th so um oh nice if you now there's a problem though which is uh so i listen to these books on audio because i they're really long um but the first book was 45 and a half hours"], [0, "The second book was 48 hours. And so the good news is if by the time you listen to this, you could in theory make it through all of the books just in time for the third book to come out. But the bad news is that's like, or the good news, I guess, depending is, I mean, that's like almost a hundred hours of audio. I don't even know how many pages that would be a lot of pages."], [0, "but a hundred hours between now and November 14th. If you want to get them all done in time for the release party for the third night in the series. Yeah. Marathon audio book. I don't know if that's been done before. So, so no. Okay. I have to say at least something about the book. So I recommend a lot of Brandon Sanderson books, I guess. I don't know. I,"], [0, "it is what it is i i enjoy his writing style uh and i i don't think i'm the only one um but basically this continues where the first book left off and that means i can't say anything about it because basically anything i say about the second book is going to be a spoiler for the first book so if you liked the first book"], [0, "This one is also really good. If you didn't like the first book, you know, I never know what to do if I read the first book of a trilogy or a series and I don't like it. It's like, well, I could go on. And it's like, well, what? Because if I read the second book, I'm going to quit two thirds of the way through. So I guess start it and plan to make it all the way through, although that's a big commitment. It'll probably, I guess by the time it's all said and done, be close to 150 hours. Wow."], [0, "Anyways, but the Words of Radiance is an endorsement if you finish the first book. And if you haven't read the first book, go read it. It's really good. It's fantasy. And I like it. And The Way of Kings, book one. Words of Radiance, book two. And the third one, I think, is going to be called Oathbringer, which I can't say why that means something. But I can't say why."], [0, "Ah, okay. No spoilers. No spoilers. Very cool. And so you can read all those books on Audible. And if you don't yet have an Audible account, you can actually go to our show notes. We have a link. It's audibletrial.com slash programming throwdown. If you go to that URL, you can actually sign up and get a free book with your subscription by clicking on that link. And also that helps us keep the show up and running."], [0, "So we also, for people who already have Audible or want to support us in another way, we have Patreon. It's getting pretty close to Christmas. So at Christmas, we take, you know, whatever extra money we have from the show and we spend it on T-shirts that we give to random people. So, you know, we don't, you could totally, what's the word? You could totally snipe the Patreon by becoming a subscriber this month."], [0, "I think we're going to come up with fair. I think we're going to have a more fair way than that."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["fantasy books", "epic fantasy", "book series", "audiobook", "brandon sanderson", "stormlight archive", "reading recommendations", "book discussion", "spoilers", "publishing", "long novels", "audible", "podcast", "patreon", "merchandise"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778145147.704125, "duration_sec": 2.314765453338623, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "I could never support that, to turn someone's water off. Corpus Christi's industrial water users, which include ExxonMobil and Valero, would also be required to cut back. The proposal needs to be approved by the City Council to go into effect. For NPR News, I'm Nina Satija in Austin. This is NPR."], [0, "Early humans lived in small communities across Africa for millennia. Climate influenced where they settled, along with disease, according to new research. Reporter Ari Daniel has more on the study. A team of researchers wondered whether malaria, a longtime lethal disease carried by mosquitoes, may have influenced where early humans lived."], [0, "So they took a set of climate models spanning the last 74,000 years, overlaid where mosquitoes would have lived, and compared that to where people were, based on archaeological evidence. The result was clear, says University of Cambridge evolutionary ecologist Andrea Manica. Basically, they were just not persisting in the areas where malaria would have been problematic."], [0, "Then, some 15,000 years ago, when the sickle cell anemia mutation arose, which can offer protection against malaria, people's avoidance of the regions with the disease began to break down. For NPR News, I'm Ari Daniel."], [0, "Air quality warnings remain in effect today across the southeastern United States from wildfires. Blazes have torn through Georgia this week, aided by drought conditions. The two biggest fires in southern Georgia have spread rapidly over the past two days and destroyed more than 50 homes. Smoke is blanketing cities hundreds of miles away."], [0, "The San Francisco Symphony has announced that its renowned music director laureate, Michael Tilson Thomas, has died of brain cancer. He was 81. Thomas was only 29 when he debuted with the San Francisco Symphony. He was a specialist in interpreting the music of Gustav Mahler, but was also a champion of American music. This is NPR News."], [0, "You know, every day on Up First, NPR's Golden Globe-nominated morning news podcast, we bring you three essential stories. At the heart of each story are questions. What really happened? What really mattered? What happens next? At NPR, we stand for your right to be curious and to follow the facts. Follow Up First wherever you get your podcasts and start your day knowing what matters and why."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["npr news", "water conservation", "drought", "city council", "industrial water use", "climate change", "wildfires", "air quality", "environmental health", "malaria", "mosquitoes", "human evolution", "archaeology", "classical music", "brain cancer"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778145336.6265697, "duration_sec": 1.9334666728973389, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And we've got the team, the team, the team for our number 2. Mike Parsons, Danielle Mason, Rich Luzinski. I'm Paul W. Smith, and we all endeavor to bring you relentless positive radio. We call upon experts to help us along the way. You can stream us, podcast us later at wjr.com, download the free WJR app, and you can even watch us now at the WJR Facebook and YouTube pages because now we've got live video streaming. Happy Ash Wednesday to you, first night of Ramadan, and we're ready to go on this story I told you about. There were up to 10 skiers missing, an avalanche in Castle Peak near Truckee, winter storm causing all kinds of trouble, and a lot of things that, that unless you're a skier, you, you may not understand. And that would include me, I don't understand. So I called upon my friend and expert, Dr. Solomon Colgan, because he is a recognized sports medicine expert and I happen to know avid backcountry skier along with his son Eli, who is a member of the Snow Basin Freeride Ski Team in Utah. This kid is such a good skier at 12, they're looking at him for future Olympics team activity. Now, there's been deep concern over the Castle Peak avalanche incident. As seasoned off-the-beaten-trail skiers, Dr. Cogan recognizes the importance of avalanche warnings, respecting safety protocols, and today's avalanche occurred during a high avalanche danger rating, conditions in which frankly no backcountry travel should occur. So they urge skiers to stay informed and wait for safer conditions. Sal, welcome back. Always a pleasure to have you on the program helping out. This is one of the more unusual ways that you're helping us."], [1, "Yeah, it sure is, Paul. How you doing today?"], [0, "I'm doing well, but I knew I could call upon you because you ski all the time and your son Eli is a world-class skier and you keep nurturing him with that every weekend to be with his ski team. Can you tell me or explain to all of us what happened at Castle Peak today?"], [1, "Well, you know, I wasn't there, but here's what I'll tell you is, you know, there was a large, large avalanche and there were avalanche warnings. You know, there was one report said 15, another said 16. I think it\u2014 I think there were\u2014 ended up being 15 guys. 9 are missing. And, you know, the avalanche level of safety warning is really, really important. You know, 1 or 2 is would be low or moderate, 3 is considerable, and then 4 and 5 is high and then extreme. I would never think about going out anywhere at high or extreme. I mean, you know, and there's other factors too that affect an avalanche, that how steep the pitch is. In other words, the steeper the, the steeper the mountain is in that spot, the more risk of avalanche. And then here's a bigger problem. They were in whiteout conditions. They had received about 2 or 3 feet of snow, you know, before that. They couldn't necessarily see very well, so you can't see where there's those, the changes in elevation or the change in pitch, and it really made for a very dangerous situation."], [0, "One that, that most of us, if we were to heed the warnings, would not have found ourselves in, although these skiers had guides with them."], [1, "They did. And, and, you know, listen, we don't know the whole situation, so, you know, they could have been there. Either way, um, it\u2014 I would not have found myself out there in that situation. And it's very sad, and we, we hope that they're okay. I understand that of the 6 that were, were found so far, 2 of them were injured, need hospitalization. But it's, it's just a scary, scary thing. And we take precaution really, really seriously. I mean, you know, one is the warning, two are the conditions, because you have to consider, Paul, not only did they get caught in this avalanche, but the amount of time it would take to get anyone there to try to rescue them. So even if you survive the avalanche and you're okay, you're out in these horrendously cold conditions with winds, and it could take many, many hours for any help to get there at all."], [0, "So somebody apparently had an emergency beacon device, which I guess you probably don't need to worry about because you won't go out in those conditions. But what is the\u2014 what is the attraction of skiing the backcountry? To me, that's a dangerous area just by the name."], [1, "Well, first of all, anyone skiing out of bounds from any resort, heli-skiing or backcountry skiing, will have have a beacon in any condition regardless of whether it's a low risk of avalanche or more considerably high. That's just part of the gear. You've got a beacon, you've got a pick, you've got a shovel, and that's very important for anyone skiing outside of a resort. But what's the attraction? You know, listen, every, every skier loves powder. And when you can get out in an area where it's unskied and fresh powder, that's super attractive to a skier. And it It can be safe, relatively speaking. I mean, skiing's a dangerous sport, but if you take all the precautions, I know many people that have done it many times, including us, and done it safely. But you really have to look at multiple factors."], [0, "Good point. And you always remind us too that you, you stress avalanche safety. You listen to what the experts are telling you. You stay away if it's a high period of time, you always should check with the official avalanche forecasts. If it's high or extreme, stay off. I mean, you have another day. Lived to, to ski another day. Anything else we need to know, Doc?"], [1, "Yeah, you know, just be careful. Listen, you, you don't want to go where\u2014 if someone's a skier, sometimes they'll rope stuff off, and there's always a reason, and it may look attractive, but there's inherent dangers. So, you know, Follow the rules. This is your life. You only get one life to live and you want to have quality time, you know, and quality time is not when you're all banged up because you did something wrong."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["avalanche", "skiing", "backcountry", "winter storm", "mountain safety", "rescue", "outdoor sports", "snow conditions", "weather warnings", "risk management", "emergency equipment", "sports medicine", "wilderness travel", "hiking", "survival"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778145389.667509, "duration_sec": 1.7482714653015137, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[1, "Year after year, Los Salgadones would continue to throw parties like that first one. Americans would return home, talk of the tacos, the town, their teeth. What was once a town of about 750 when Maga\u00f1a first arrived is now crammed with clinics, hotels, restaurants, and pharmacies. Today, young dentists and souvenir vendors from all over Mexico continue to migrate to town in hopes of finding stable work. And the tourists keep coming in droves."], [2, "In winter, it's too much people and they make lines, and that's uncomfortable for them, but they don't care. They make the lines, you know, and they still coming because they find dental services good, a good price and everything, and very handsome doctors. We live from the tourists, you know."], [1, "We live from the tourists. It's a sentiment I heard from everyone in town. Teenagers will run to the border after school to sell trinkets to people waiting in line to go home. Women will walk up and down the line selling handmade sweaters and hot meals. And on the American side, the Quechan Tribe manages a casino resort. It's a popular spot for dental tourists who try their hand at the slot machines in between appointments."], [3, "I think Agritones is a good town. It's become a better town than it was before. You know, we really don't have a lot of, like, scenic views or, like, anything touristic. But the people are really, like, they're really, like, hardworking people. And they're also, they like to party, you know? They like to have good times also."], [1, "Street promoter Alberto moved back to the area 8 years ago, in part to be closer to his family after a road accident in Canc\u00fan made it difficult for him to walk. He told me he gets discounted dental care from the clinics that employ him, but he's been putting off amputating his leg for years because of the cost of surgery."], [3, "For molars, we vary anywhere from $120 to some of us\u2014 some of us are making like $500 or $800 a week."], [1, "I asked Alberto what he thought about the town's transformation into Molar City."], [3, "I think dental has helped Algodones a lot, but I think people just make their money and they go somewhere else, you know? They go live it up somewhere else. Because a lot of these doctors have become millionaires."], [1, "Many of those original dentists live in mansions complete with indoor courtyards and pools. In addition to their dental practice, certain families in town own many of the hotels, restaurants, and pharmacies here. But zoom out of the town center and you'll see mostly unpaved roads and humble homes. There's this 18th-century paleontologist, Georges Cuvier. He has this famous quote about teeth. \"Show me your teeth,\" he said, \"and I will tell you who you are.\" It's true. That from teeth alone, a dental anthropologist can gather all sorts of information. They can decipher migration patterns and cultural norms. They can tell if you ate with your fingers or if you endured famine. Our teeth are biographies. They hold facts about ourselves, facts we may not even recognize while we're alive. I'm curious how you would describe Your relationship to your teeth?"], [3, "You know, my relationship with my teeth was, you know, I didn't really take the time on a daily basis to have good dental care. I haven't truly smiled in probably 10 years."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "nam