{"timestamp": 1778149802.9159913, "duration_sec": 1.9087624549865723, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "So you're going to try and force your latent distribution to be relatively close to a mean of zero and a standard deviation of one. And so finally, before we can start training our variational autoencoder, we have one final trick that we have to use. Because if you look at the computation graph of our network right now, we have a problem. In the middle of that network, after the bottleneck, we have a sampling operation. There is a node there that takes a sample from a distribution and then feeds that sample through the decoder."], [0, "But the problem is that you cannot run back propagation, you cannot push gradients through a sampling node. So this is an issue. And so in order to run your gradients through the entire network and train everything end to end, we're going to use what we call the reparameterization trick. And so the trick goes as follows. If you look at the latent vector that you're sampling, you can actually look at that vector as the sum of a fixed mu, which is just the parameter that you're learning, plus some kind of a sigma, which is also a parameter that you're learning,"], [0, "and then multiply it with an epsilon, and this epsilon is where we're gonna put the stochastic part. So this epsilon is always going to be standard caution, it's always gonna have zero mean and standard deviation of one. We're gonna sample from that epsilon, and then multiply it with sigma, add mu, and we have our latent vector."], [0, "And so the clever thing here is that now our mu and our sigma, those are the only things that we actually want to train. So there we have to be able to compute gradients and run backpropagation. But that epsilon, well, that doesn't really matter because we don't want to change that epsilon ever again. That epsilon is a fixed stochastic node. Okay, it's still stochastic, but we don't have to run backpropagation through it. So it doesn't matter that it's a sampling operation."], [0, "And so this is the reparameterization trick, where instead of having a full stochastic node that is blocking all of your gradients because you can't do backpropagation through it, you're going to split it up into a part where you can do backprop, and then another part which is still stochastic, but which you don't want to train because it's fixed. Pretty clever, right? Once again, that was the very excellent Archive Insights channel, which we felt was the best explanation of VAEs and the reparameterization trick we could find."], [0, "However, to really understand VAEs, there is a lot of math and code that we are skimming over for the sake of time."], [0, "Check the show notes for more helpful videos. We return to ICLR to hear Dirk Kingma talk about his 10-year retrospective on the VAE in his Test of Time Award speech. This work is great because it brought ideas from deep learning and probabilistic models together and introduced many novel techniques such as amortized inference, reparametrization trick, and the use of lower bound to jointly optimize both the encoder and decoder."], [0, "In fact, the ideas are so good that two groups of people came up with it at the same time. So there's another paper on this concurrent work shortly after, proposed by Danilo Rezende, Shakir Mohamed, and Dain Vastra, which was published in ICML 2014. Maybe they'll also get a Test on Time Award in the same conference venue. So, well, now, 10 years after, looking back, we can all agree that"], [0, "This was a great contribution and we are very happy to have this paper as the first ever inaugural test of time award for iClear. So thanks to both the authors and the other co-inventors and everybody else who's been using these things to make this really great. And this has given a lot of progress. Lots of things have come out of this great work. So looking forward to your talk. So please take it away."], [0, "All right, so let me first say that it's an absolutely huge honor to get the first ever Test of Time Award. So thank you very much to the conference chairs as well as the award committee to award this to us. I'll quickly start because we have a lot to say. So first a little bit of history. Let me kick it off with a few historical notes. Of course, a very key important part of the variational autoencoder is the encoder, the autoencoder, in fact."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["variational autoencoder", "deep learning", "machine learning", "probabilistic models", "latent space", "distribution", "sampling", "reparameterization", "backpropagation", "gradients", "encoder decoder", "inference", "optimization", "research conference", "neural networks"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778150137.424104, "duration_sec": 1.6772527694702148, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Was on Friar Street."], [1, "Yep, yep, yep."], [0, "Right down from the Goosens."], [1, "Yep. Yeah, right down."], [0, "Yeah."], [1, "And, um, that, that was an honor, man, to be able to train in that gym. That was incredible. It was incredible."], [0, "Oh yeah, yeah, I used to be there. Yeah, yeah, man. Yeah, those are some great times because, I mean, I connect with Benny where\u2014 because when I was in Bridgeport, my instructor Manny Malice went to California and started training with Benny. Oh wow, they're early on. So he put that on the map about coming to the mecca and training with Benny."], [1, "It was the mecca for kickboxing, especially in the '90s. That was the mecca. You had to go to the Jet Center."], [0, "Everyone talked about the Jet Center. Yeah, man, that was\u2014 So we were."], [1, "Always wondering, like, what is the style? What's the best style? And then the UFC came along and I'm like, oh my God, they did it. They did it. They figured it out. They put it all together. And for a while it was jiu-jitsu because nobody understood jiu-jitsu and Royce Gracie was just running shit."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["martial arts", "kickboxing", "gym training", "combat sports", "mixed martial arts", "ufc", "jiu jitsu", "grappling", "fighting styles", "sports history", "boxing", "athletic training", "california", "1990s"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
