{"timestamp": 1778153309.7368946, "duration_sec": 1.4089717864990234, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[2, "This was my first party. Look at American people, how they doing just in the street, you know? I don't have places to do it."], [1, "Medina flips through the book fondly, as if he's cooing at baby photos."], [2, "And that's the way Algodones start, you know, with that. And like this, you can see all my book is full of parties, you know, to where I go and everything, you know. Well, it's all my life in this book, you know."], [1, "It's clear that Medina put his heart into these parties, into making the town grow the way it did. He convinced his neighbors to cook enough food to feed thousands for free. He hired a mariachi band and converted an old boxing dressing ring into a stage. And the Americans had a great time. Medina made sure the margaritas were strong."], [2, "And they was drunk like crazy, you know. The American customs told me, Dr. Medina, why you give to those guys?"], [0, "Why?"], [2, "They cross right here, they don't want to show the papers, they tell us son of a bitch and go."], [3, "They was very drunk."], [1, "Year after year, Los Salgadones would continue to throw parties like that first one. Americans would return home, talk of the tacos, the town, their teeth. What was once a town of about 750 when Maga\u00f1a first arrived is now crammed with clinics, hotels, restaurants, and pharmacies. Today, young dentists and souvenir vendors from all over Mexico continue to migrate to town in hopes of finding stable work. And the tourists keep coming in droves."], [2, "In winter, it's too much people and they make lines, and that's uncomfortable for them, but they don't care. They make the lines, you know, and they still coming because they find dental services good, a good price and everything, and very handsome doctors. We live from the tourists, you know."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["tourism", "mexico", "border town", "american visitors", "party", "festival", "food", "tacos", "margaritas", "mariachi", "local economy", "dental care", "dentistry", "small business", "migration"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778153597.4982495, "duration_sec": 2.335519790649414, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Needless to say, this year has been a little bit more than a spike, but like a huge ramp up from new customers on both sides of the political spectrum are buying things up. And it hasn't gone away yet. How many months has this been going on? And people are still buying and the industry is still like,"], [0, "We don't know if we want to ramp up production yet. I'm sure some have. But it's crazy and it's like, where is it going to go? How is this going to... This has been the most unknown..."], [0, "future for the firearm industry. And I don't know where they're going. The good news is, well, it's who you ask is the good news, but I don't think, I think they played it smart on their part. And I don't think they're going anywhere because demand is good. And I don't think they're going to lose that demand anytime soon. So they may ramp it up, the production up slowly, but yeah, that's my only economic comment on any kind of manufacturing right now."], [0, "Yeah, the economics around firearms is very, very complex because I agree with you that the price of a product does depend on its availability. You've got different tiers of firearms and all the accessories and bullets also. It'll take forever to catch up trying to buy ammunition. Yeah."], [0, "That's a problem that the industry has seen over and over again. It's a problem that they allow the market to fix itself, basically, to self-correct. And I think that's the general approach that they're trying to play is that they will maintain production rates or assess risks on if they do try and scale up, right? What's the cost of scaling up and can they maintain the supply and demand or maintain that price at that new supply rate?"], [0, "So I think they'll allow the market to kind of fix itself unless something else does happen. Because right now we do have the U.S. government looking for new assault rifles, new ammunition, which could play into or could phase out existing ammunitions that they're purchasing or change demand for existing firearms. So there's a lot. That's wild that you bring that up because I love how, you know, the rest of the firearm industry is just like, okay,"], [0, "can we keep up with the demand and is this demand gonna last and it's proving right now that the demand is lasting right so it's driving up the prices because the industry's like we don't know if we're supposed to ramp up because you know we don't want this demand to just disappear all of a sudden and now we're over producing and now we're going into surplus and now we're going out of business yeah so and and and they've been really smart with that regard"], [0, "out of a business standpoint, that's really smart. From a consumer standpoint, it was like, no, this is awful. I need to go do my mag dumps this weekend. It's funny that the industry is just focused on keeping up right now."], [0, "You know, should we keep up with demand? Should we ramp up to keep up with demand right now? Or should we just keep doing what we're doing? And the smart thing is keep doing what you're doing. Meanwhile, here comes the whole U.S. military. And it's like, uh..."], [0, "Yeah, we need some research and development done. We want new weapons. We want a new caliber. We want new weapons. We want all of the new technology. And these guys, the rest of the industry is just like, we're just trying to keep up right now. We don't want to do anything new. Get out of here with that."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Technology"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0