{"timestamp": 1778155109.2005665, "duration_sec": 2.95070743560791, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "He\u2014 why am I confused with\u2014 what's."], [1, "His real\u2014 Donnie Brasco's real name?"], [0, "Joe\u2014 I'm not confused with Bronx Tale."], [1, "That's not Bronx. Joe Pistone."], [0, "That's not nothing to do with Bronx Tale, right?"], [1, "No, different story. That's a different story. Yeah. Um, so Donnie Brasco was the guy who was\u2014 he was an agent and he pretended to be a mob guy, and he got in with the mob and was with him for like 7 years, did all kinds of shit with the mob, and then sold everybody out and they all went under."], [0, "Yeah, I don't know, I'm just so\u2014 did this\u2014 the culture, everything is There used to be a phrase, snitches get stitches. Now, I don't know if this just everywhere, but everywhere I go, it's like the most interesting thing now that's selling on any platform, especially social media, is beef. And I don't understand why people gravitate toward negativity more than anything. That's normal. Why? But you know what? It's interesting. Your platform is not known for that. No. Your platform, but I go to these other platforms and I I think these guys, these people, they just sit back and it's like, okay, what can I say to make people upset, get them riled up, and then I take advantage of the engagement that they do."], [1, "They're in a different game than me. All right, their game is trying to get engagement. My game is talking to people that are interested."], [0, "Doesn't that become, I don't want to be triggering or anything, but doesn't that."], [1, "Become destructive after a while?"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["organized crime", "mafia", "undercover agent", "informant", "crime film", "donnie brasco", "joe pistone", "mob culture", "social media", "online engagement", "internet drama", "negativity", "conflict", "media ethics", "platform culture"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778156208.9392078, "duration_sec": 2.267967939376831, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Talk about Becky Shaw. I mean, have you always wanted to pursue Broadway? Is this something you have dreamt of as well? Oh, yeah, yeah. I mean, I'm a theater kid. I'm a theater kid at heart. The pit is my first try at TV, but I spent 10, 15 years working in the theater all across America, and I never got the chance to do Broadway. I never got the chance to come to the big show. And so this 16-year-old me is kicking his feet right now. Wow. Yeah."], [0, "You've gotten some interesting gifts at the stage door while you've been there on Broadway. Is that? Yeah. What are people bringing to you? Yeah, well, here I brought a couple. Oh. So Dr. Langdon in season one wears a dad bracelet that his kid made him. And now people have really latched onto that. So every night whenever I come out to the stage door, I have a bunch of fans come out and make me these really special, funny little friendship bracelets."], [0, "That's cool. I love friendship bracelet. You guys know I love a friendship bracelet. Well, we got a baby Jane Doe, which is one of my favorites. That's hilarious. This is yours if you want it. Wow. Look at that. I don't think you know how much I like friendship bracelets. She's a junkie when it comes to the bracelets. From Dr. Langdon, too."], [0, "By the way, we have some for you as well. And some crocs, too. No! Oh, yes. Oh, yes. Oh, yes. This is amazing. Oh, man. I cannot tell you how much this means. I am a croc guy through and through. This is iconic. But they're perfect for the hospital. Exactly. And they are perfect for... I can literally do anything in crocs. I can climb a mountain in crocs. I can rehearse all of Hamlet in crocs. It's true."], [0, "You can wear them at Becky Shaw. There you go. This is amazing. Another right sock. Congratulations. Patrick, thanks so much for stopping by. I really appreciate it. Thank you so much for having me. And don't forget, Becky Shaw playing now at Second Stage's Hayes Theater. And don't forget, Catch the Pit Thursday nights on HBO Max. He's excited about this crap."], [0, "Coming up, we actually have a real doctor in the house to help us make sense of our lab results. He's also going to tell us the important numbers that we should be looking out for. And then later, the hilarious David Cross, live here to fill us in on his new comedy special. He's reading a book of dad jokes, that comedy special that I watched yesterday. No dad jokes in that one. Oh, no. Third hour of today, right back after this. I'll tell your dad those jokes. No."], [0, "Well, this morning on today's checklist, we're going to talk about the importance of routine blood work and making sense of those sometimes confusing lab results. And here to help us do that, board certified surgeon, Dr. Cedric McFadden. Welcome back, Dr. McFadden. Good morning. Thank you. So first of all, how often should the average person, person of average health, how often should they be getting these routine blood labs and what numbers should they be looking at?"], [0, "Yeah, so for most of us, we're going to be doing this blood work every one to two years. However, depending on your medical history, you may be doing it more frequently. So if you have chronic diseases like heart disease or diabetes, you may be doing this more frequently. Also, the number, obviously, it's important, the cadence that we do this. What's even more important is the change that we see in these numbers."], [0, "If there is a shift in the baseline that you normally around, that becomes really more telling. And particularly, what we want to remember is that if there is a change from your baseline, we want to see if it's going up, is it going down? Is the abnormal labs that we're seeing, are they related to other symptoms that you could be having at the time those labs are drawn? We don't treat you just based on a single number. Yeah."], [0, "We treat them based on how you're doing. Are there other medications that you're taking? Did you eat before you take this lab? Because, again, that can influence the results. Okay. My friend Emily has a few questions. Okay, so I feel like we get blood work done, and so often we're looking normal, abnormal. Should we be looking beyond that? What does normal even mean?"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["broadway", "theater", "acting", "stage door", "fans", "television", "medical", "blood work", "lab results", "health screening", "routine checkups", "chronic disease", "diabetes", "heart disease", "comedy"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778156221.3429942, "duration_sec": 1.8179676532745361, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Where do I host this thing? Where do I run it? And all of my enthusiasm for building this thing kind of just goes out the window. I'm like, actually, you know what? I'm fine doing this manual task. And one of the things that we saw internally as we were building prototypes is we were building a lot of these kinds of integrations. One of the things that we see people building a ton of both, you know, obviously within the company, but in our user base,"], [0, "is these Slack integrations, which are really fun, really easy to build, and made all the more powerful when you get to connect all the data that you care about. So a colleague of mine built this really neat Slack chatbot using Google's Dialogflow"], [0, "where he always gets confused in AWS CloudWatch about how to specify queries and whether it's UTC or local time zone and all that stuff. And so he built a bot where he can chat with it and say, hey, can you find the log"], [0, "this error, either in our staging environment or our production environment. And then Dialogflow, which is its own kind of magic, parses that NLP and then hands back to transposite the key nouns and verbs. And then his app goes and fetches the appropriate data out of CloudWatch. So we see a lot of those kinds of automating quality of life applications being built."], [0, "Unrelated to any of this, because you shared in the chat the documentation for MailChimp, I really like that you guys are using the native details and summary elements for your expand and collapse functionality on your website. Oh, cool. Developments being used for their intended purpose."], [0, "Yeah, good. That was done, you know, a day or two ago or whatever. So also that might be up your alley. We're using Eleventy. So I want to give a shout out to Eleventy. Oh, awesome. Yeah, Zach's static site generator. That's awesome. We're embarrassingly excited about it. Yeah, JS Jabber is hosted on Eleventy now too. So that's..."], [0, "Yeah, my 11pm to 2am project is usually all Eleventy based. That's awesome. Yeah, it's an awesome platform. I'd love to learn a little bit more about how this"], [0, "actually works under the hood. This seems like a huge undertaking. I know we've talked a little bit about Swagger slash LAS or OpenAPI specification, but what sort of other things do you need to make something like this work under the hood? Yeah, good question. And yeah, there are a ton of different pieces."], [0, "So first, part of the core of this thing is what we call the relational engine. It's this parser that takes the SQL and translates it into this intermediate form and executes it efficiently. So that's one big chunk of that. Our backend is all in Java and don't hate us for it, but we picked it kind of because it's boring."], [0, "And we have this very pragmatic attitude internally. I love PHP for the same reason. It's boring and it works. Yeah, exactly. You know, I think I made the mistake of telling my co-founder, you know, people like Java, but nobody loves Java. And it turns out she actually is in love with Java."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided 