{"timestamp": 1778160128.0417461, "duration_sec": 2.003631591796875, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "I mean, how old were you before somebody outside that system told you what was actually happening in your home wasn't okay? Give us this story of how they basically normalized the not normal."], [0, "So I was 17. I was almost 18. And just for context, my father was a very, was a very talented pianist. And so he was very well liked, very well known, very well respected, had traveled and played for a lot of people, including Carl Stevens himself back in the day. But he had significant anger issues and they knew it. And all my life, he would get angry sometimes."], [0, "over anything like we never knew what it was going to be and that particular late spring early summer he had gotten angry with me over what I don't really remember he had pinned me against the refrigerator in our in our apartment and he was right in my face screaming at me"], [0, "which at this point in my life, like him being physical was new. I don't recall before him that him being physical with me, but him screaming at me was definitely not new. And so I really didn't think a whole lot of it. I was out a few days later with my boyfriend and we were in his pickup truck riding down the road. It's hot. I've got a T-shirt on. The sleeve moved and he saw a bruise. I didn't realize that my dad had left circular bruises on my upper arms from pinning me against the fridge."], [0, "And he saw the bruises and was like, what is that? And so, of course, I look and I, oh, well, my dad got mad the other day. And, you know, he he he helped me and he was screaming at me. And I said it like it was no big deal, because to me at this point, it wasn't. He pulled the truck over and asked to look at the other arm. And he just looked at me and he said, that is completely not normal. And it is not OK."], [0, "that he did that to you. And that's the first time anybody had actually said that, that this is not normal behavior, this is not appropriate behavior, and this cannot continue. Yeah. You know, I didn't realize until much later in life, he actually went and confronted my father and told him straight up he would not be doing that again, or he would report him. So, I mean, how is it being handled internally there?"], [0, "Oh, they sent they sent them to different pastors, most of whom were not trained to handle this kind of situation. And to be fair, not only did they not give my they didn't do my family any favors, they didn't do my dad any favors either. I mean, they let him go unchecked with all these issues for so long."], [0, "In the end, he lost out on a lot in terms of relationships with us, with his family, because of his issues that just went untreated and unchecked. And he didn't ever feel like he had to. And why should he? They weren't going to do anything about it because they cared more about his piano playing skills than they did about him as a person, which is really sad when you think about it."], [0, "Was it more of a liability, in your opinion, that if it were to come out that he's doing this, it's not only just his piano playing skills, it's, well, it's a member of our church that's at a higher position that's doing it. It would reflect negatively on the group as a whole. So let's insulate this. And obviously, and not that it excuses it by any means, but..."], [0, "I don't want to say you can understand why, but if you're operating from a place of depravity and then not looking out for people, you can understand why that that would be the choice they would make. It's not the right one. But if you don't care about the well-being and you're more about image, that's the route someone's going to take. Is this the route that they would take, in your opinion, at that place and time?"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["domestic abuse", "family violence", "emotional abuse", "physical abuse", "trauma", "child welfare", "relationship dynamics", "religious institutions", "church leadership", "pastoral counseling", "accountability", "institutional cover up", "power and authority", "mental health", "anger management"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778160934.89271, "duration_sec": 2.5481817722320557, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Hello, everyone. Thank you to all of you who listened to the first episode of the This Week in Rust podcast last week. It's great to bring you another one this week for issue number 340. I'm Nell Shamrell-Harrington, lead editor of This Week in Rust, and an engineer on the Rust team at Mozilla."], [0, "Every week, this podcast will highlight a few of the stories from this week's issue of This Week in Rust. While the newsletter and the podcast are usually published on Tuesdays, due to the U.S. holiday on Monday of this week, we are publishing on Wednesday. Let's dive into this week's highlights."], [0, "One of the more interesting stories this week is a blog post by Dennis Studah on his blog, seri.tools. He figured out how to compile Rust binaries for the Windows 98 second edition operating system and more."], [0, "Even if you don't anticipate running on Windows 98 SE at any point in the near future, it's a fascinating lesson in backporting Rust for an older operating system. And also in understanding how Rust builds for different targeted operating systems and how it works under the surface. Definitely check it out."], [0, "In related news, this week's issue also features a story by Stephen Sherratt. He wrote a fascinating blog post about creating a Rust program which produced a Nintendo Entertainment System binary that ran Conway's Game of Life in an NES emulator. It is fascinating to see all the different operating systems that Rust can create programs to run on. Something I've always enjoyed with This Week in Rust is following multi-part blog posts as they progress week after week."], [0, "This week, we feature the final post in the Writing Python Inside Your Rust Code series by Mara Boss, which is a great series, and the first post in the Zero to Production series by Luca Palmieri on building production-ready APIs in Rust. I'm looking forward to more posts from that series in the future."], [0, "If you have ever wondered how to organize your Rust tests better, Andre Bogus has written just the post for you. This post, called, appropriately, how to organize your Rust tests, takes you through when to assert, dual-use doc tests, how library crates can lead by example, verifying that things fail the right way, testing randomly, and more. Give it a read to level up your Rust testing skills."], [0, "If you are not sure how to use macro rules or just need a review, Sven Osman has written a post called Rust Macro Rules in Practice. It is the first in a series from him called Practical Rust Bites that showcases tiny pieces of rust taken out of real projects. Give it a read for a very clear introduction and practical explanation of macros in Rust."], [0, "We have several videos featured in this week's issue, including Bringing Web Assembly to the Web with WASI, the Web Assembly System Interface by Lynn Clark, and Microsoft Safe Systems Programming Languages effort by Dean Bryan and Ryan Levick. There are several great beginning videos on iteration by Tim McNaira, and even more fantastic videos in the newsletter. Definitely give them a watch."], [0, "Want to get started in contributing to Rust open source projects but haven't been sure how to start? The call for participation section features issues from pajama, mdbxrs, ruma, timers, and http types. Click on them and take a look and hopefully you'll be motivated to work through them and get that pull request in."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["rust", "systems programming", "programming languages", "compiler", "cross compilation", "legacy systems", "windows", "nintendo", "emulation", "game of life", "testing", "macros", "webassembly", "api development", "open source"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
