{"timestamp": 1778161500.8625407, "duration_sec": 2.4094512462615967, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "This is Hidden Killers with Tony Brewski. Here now, Tony Brewski. 22 days, well, 23 days, I think, as of right now. Who's keeping track, though? The footage is everywhere. That grainy image of a masked man on Nancy Guthrie's Porsche has been broadcast nationally, shared millions of times, dissected on every platform. The FBI is showing photos to gun shop owners. Walmart is handing over purchase records."], [0, "For the Ozark Trail backpack, genetic genealogy is processing DNA. If this person is local and the January reconnaissance suggests they might be, they're not hiding in a bunker. They're living their life while watching the walls close in. Robin Drake, retired FBI special agency for the Counterintelligence Behavioral Analysis Program, is with us. Let's get into more about what might be going on with this individual today."], [0, "if they're still out there somewhere. They've spent now almost four weeks watching themselves become the most wanted person in America. What does that sustained psychological pressure actually do to someone? I mean, how does it change their behavior in ways that people around them might be noticing?"], [0, "So a lot depends. It's the worst answer always is it depends. And it does depend. It depends if they're watching it or not. Because if they're laying so low that they're not paying attention or they don't have access to social media, they don't have access to TV, they're not impacted very much by it at all, as well as the people around them."], [0, "But here's an interesting observation, though, on this one. In general, yes, people are going to always have the weight of the stress on them of what's going on and always being in fight or flight. That has a massive physiological impact on our bodies, a very negative one over a sustained period of time. And so in general, yes, that kind of sustained pressure will start cracking. It will start ebbing out with all the behaviors of people around them. It becomes extremely notable."], [0, "Here's a few interesting things, though, with that. And the first one is just this person's demeanor on that front porch. Remember, there hasn't been a living soul that has covered this that didn't say it looked fluid. He looked comfortable doing it. It looks like he has some reps doing this."], [0, "And so that shows that he was not stressed in that moment on the front porch. And so there's a possibility that's just his demeanor. He doesn't get stressed about these things, which would not give them a lot of leakage outside of that thing, even with the enhanced intention on him. Also, if there's if what happens that I did, the research I did on this one was the levels of connectivity because the sheriff and others put out there that they're not ruling out other accomplices."], [0, "Compass is an interesting word. People knowledgeable maybe of his lifestyle of being a burglar, breaking an entry, things like this. There might be people aware of that. And so his pattern could be, well, he does a burglary once a week. And so once a week, he comes back stressing. In other words, if it's a normal pattern behavior for breaking an entry and the people around him know that, it's going to fall in that normal pattern of behavior. So it won't necessarily be standing out. Then you also have..."], [0, "three types of individuals that might be aware of him. And those are family members that are potentially involved, accomplices or just waiting. You have friends and associates, which is that next level. And then you have more relative strangers that you just more like a hired hand. And at each level,"], [0, "their willingness to hide, corroborate, and back you is obviously declining. So the closest one to family members, their likelihood of them turning you in or cracking in some way or being alerted to, granted, they're the ones that are gonna be most alerted to your behavior, but if it's a pattern of things you've done before, it might look normal, even, it gets tricky, but yeah, it just gets tricky."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["true crime", "fbi investigation", "manhunt", "burglary", "dna evidence", "genetic genealogy", "surveillance footage", "suspect behavior", "criminal psychology", "stress response", "accomplices", "law enforcement", "counterintelligence", "media coverage", "social media"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778161640.9163797, "duration_sec": 2.028014659881592, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "This is the thing. Most businesses are not fucking Coca-Cola. They don't have this secret recipe that's the foundation of their success. Even, I'd probably argue at this point, I'm not even sure that's true for Coca-Cola. But let's just assume for a moment that it is, that the secret recipe or the secret ingredients... It was funny. I was just watching Kung Fu Panda the other day, and they have this like, oh, I'm going to tell you the secret ingredients to my special noodle soup. And the secret ingredients is..."], [0, "Nothing. That was the big revelation, right? Like just this aura of like, we have the secret sauce here. And a lot of companies have a secret source when it comes to the code bases. They think it's so precious. It's not precious. Vast majority of code is not precious at all. And it's not that unique. There's nothing there to steal. It is based in..."], [0, "a old style thinking based on patents and so on. And maybe that is relevant to some kinds of businesses in some ways. The vast majority of businesses, no, not relevant to them. The vast majority of businesses succeed or fail on the basis of their execution and their timing and sharing the recipes of how they got there. It doesn't do anything. There just aren't that many profound secrets that completely alter the trajectory of"], [0, "Wherever some company is going, and if competitors learn that, they'll have everything. Again, maybe that's true in other domains that we don't have any insight into, but it certainly isn't true for us. And when it's not true, you got to realize that all these lessons that sit unshared, it's just dead weight."], [0, "They earn you nothing. They earn you no goodwill. They earn you no attention. They don't do anything. It's just waste. So why don't you take that waste and turn it into some fuel? Turn it into something else. It doesn't actually take that much effort. That's the other thing. I get this all the time. How do you have time for all this sharing? How do you have time to write all these blog posts? I'm like, dude,"], [0, "I spent fucking 10 minutes writing this post and then I just hit send. It's not perfect. I didn't go over it with a copywriter. This stuff we're doing right now. Do we do five takes to get it just perfect? Just right? No, we don't. We hit record and then we go. So if you treat it at that level that these the wrapping doesn't matter that much, the production values don't need to be like a Hollywood blockbuster. The most important thing that you're sharing is"], [0, "What you learned, it doesn't take that much more. And you can get good at it, good at it in a sense that it really feels like it takes nothing at all. To me, I feel like most days, even when I'm writing or we're recording this podcast or something, it doesn't feel like it steals anything. I still have time to do all the other stuff and work on the products and do everything else that we can do. So it's not like you suddenly have to think, oh, I need another job on top of the job I have just to share the things that I've learned. Absolutely not."], [0, "Okay. So you guys are very open about sharing, teaching. Are there any things that you wouldn't share? I mean, I hear you guys talk about numbers that I'm like, oh, I get surprised when I hear things like, oh, they said that. They said that number out loud. Is there anything you wouldn't share? Yeah. I mean, we typically don't share specific revenue numbers, exact customer counts. It's like..."], [0, "That kind of stuff is, I think, for us. That's one of the things that's really nice about being a private company is that you can sort of keep some of that to yourself. There's something there. And there's also not specific lessons in that. So I think most of the things that we share are lesson-based. So to say we have exactly this revenue,"], [0, "It doesn't really send... There's no lesson in that. We have said we generate tens of millions of dollars in annual profits. We'll say that, right? Now, what is the lesson there? The lesson there is that you can build a company like that. You can build a profitable company with fewer than 100 people selling software as a service the way we do it without very much marketing. But the specific number is not that interesting. So we kind of don't share those things. I think most of the things we share are lesson-based information."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["business strategy", "company culture", "open sharing", "transparency", "intellectual property", "software development", "codebase", "blogging", "content creation", "entrepreneurship", "startup growth", "private company", "revenue metrics", "execution", "software as a service"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
