ited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["seattle city council", "seattle city hall", "joy hollingsworth", "dionne foster", "debora juarez", "robert kettle", "eddie lin", "alexis mercedes rinck", "rob saka", "dan strauss", "maritza rivera", "seattle city charter"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778163225.142853, "duration_sec": 3.0685858726501465, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "We're going to the moon kind of thing. And I think it's that delusion that you are outside of the realm of basic economics that not only is false, but is harmful in terms of teaching you the things that you should know to ultimately long-term become a good business. That the fundamental principles of making more money than you spend and so forth are..."], [0, "habits, as Jason liked to say, that you have to teach yourself early on because you can't just pick them up later. And there's countless stories of startups that stayed in that immature state and never converted to a mature state where they actually had"], [0, "proper concept of making more money than they spend. And then suddenly found themselves without the guardian angels of capital. And then like, Oh, all of a sudden, like, Oh, it's the, what was it called the last time that the ice winter in 2012 or 13 or something, a bunch of BCs coined this term, rip the good times. And you're like, um, yeah,"], [0, "It's just kind of sad to me that there's that level of infantilization that, like, unless mommy and daddy continue to clear your credit card bill, exactly, you're going to be in real trouble."], [0, "How about you just stand on your own two feet and how about you just fucking move out and become independent and in charge of your own destiny? And that's the kind of stuff that you will achieve if you adhere to these basic tenets of business. And as we've talked about several times, isn't that why you're in this in the first place?"], [0, "Isn't that the purpose of starting a either business or startup? That you want some independence? That you want to run things by yourself? That you want to have your own company? Why are you moving back in with mommy and daddy capital? Having some higher aspirations here, which is part of the inversion of the ethos here, right? A lot of people adopt the word startup because it implies this limitless ambition, right? That we're not for just going and"], [0, "orbit, we're going to the moon. And yet at the same time, you don't even have the ambition to get your own house in order and to move out and be independent and in control of that destiny of yourself. There's some weird schism here to me in presenting yourself as this extremely ambitious business while at the same time not being in control of your own destiny."], [0, "As Jason was saying earlier in this conversation, I still hear Basecamp described as a startup 20 years later. Did you ever think of Basecamp or I guess 37 Signals at the time as a startup? Yeah, it was just always a business. We're starting a business now."], [0, "Back in the day, it was three of us starting a business. And that was that. You start a business and you start doing work. At the time, we were finding clients and they were paying us for the work. We were delivering the work and we were sitting around a desk and doing the work. That's what it is. And that's all it was. And all it still is. We're a business. We make stuff and we work together and we deliver things for customers. We take care of customers. We take care of employees. We do the things that a business does. There's nothing you can do that a startup does that a business doesn't do. Right."], [0, "So there's no, I just have never thought about it in any other way."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["startups", "small business", "entrepreneurship", "business fundamentals", "cash flow", "profitability", "spending", "budgeting", "financial discipline", "venture capital", "bootstrapping", "independence", "business strategy", "sustainability"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778163382.1050136, "duration_sec": 2.147022008895874, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yes."], [1, "The Bomb Squad was the first place that Eddie taught at and then that place closed down, then we went to Legends. Yeah. And then we moved to the other Legends that was like in more East LA. Mm-hmm. And then And then Eddie started opening up his own place in downtown."], [0, "Yeah, yeah. And that's where I would train with Josh Barnett at that place. Yeah, quite a bit. Yeah, yeah, man."], [1, "The old days."], [0, "Yes, man. Yeah. And who's who would be coming through the gym? Because I was training Bob Sapp at one time. And then that's how I got Frankie Lyles connected into that."], [1, "Wow."], [0, "That whole thing."], [1, "And so I remember Frankie. Frankie used to be at the Bomb Squad first."], [0, "Right. Yeah. Frankie was like my best friend in the world. You know, he's super middleweight champion of the world. That's who got me deeply into boxing. And so I would always be at his training camps and, you know, I got to train with like Tommy Hearns and all these amazing people like Sugar Ray and all these guys, man."], [1, "Frankie's a great boxing coach."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["martial arts", "boxing", "fighting", "combat sports", "gym training", "coaching", "professional athletes", "training camp", "boxing coach", "boxing champions", "mixed martial arts", "los angeles", "sports history", "athlete networking"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
