{"timestamp": 1778167377.4869604, "duration_sec": 1.45853853225708, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Team develops a Wi-Fi technology that can effectively read emotions. This shit is crazy. So it's like Wi-Fi. I'm not sure exactly what frequency. I think it's like five gigahertz. But this is a broadcast, an RF signal that is broadcasting, but it's bouncing off your face and your body and everything. And based on the bounces and based on the interpretations coming off of it,"], [0, "it can tell what your emotions are using machine learning and, of course, the bouncing or the visual of your face based on the RF combined with analysis of what that visual looks like. So imagine, well, again, that's how I end up going over an hour imagining things about these stories."], [0, "Well, I'm going to do it anyway. So imagine you walk into a room and the room is a place where it's being monitored. It's being monitored for terrorists, for example. So they're looking for high stress. They're looking for people freaking out about whatever. And they can sort of pick out a terrorist from the crowd or whatever. All sorts of problems with false negative, false positive. Someone can be good at hiding their emotions and they go through. Other people can be stressed out because they just lost a girlfriend or a boyfriend or whatever."], [0, "So all sorts of possibilities there, but holy crap. I mean, you could have monitored areas, scanning your face, you don't even know it. And somewhere on a screen, you're being registered as the emotion that you're feeling at that time, if you're not properly concealing it. Fascinating stuff."], [0, "The Google Now brand has been killed off and replaced with Google Assistant. And the new chat app, I can't remember the name of it, Allo or Alio or something like that, is the first app that's come out. So it's a chat app, but you can actually use Google Assistant to interact with it. I think it's a great move. It's basically Assistant is what you would think of. You would not think of now, like you don't."], [0, "contact your now person, you contact your assistant. And I think it's what people will want in the future. So I think it's a good brand change. Researchers maintain a light photon quantum link across six kilometers. So they basically have a cable stretched across six kilometers, although I heard seven. But you can basically, and I'm going to mangle this because it's not my area of expertise, but"], [0, "It's the concept from Ender's Game where you basically separate photons at a very primitive state. And as far as you can separate them, a state change on one side will affect the other side instantaneously. So in Ender's Game, this was called the Ansible, and it's how they did direct instantaneous communication with"], [0, "over vast distances, you know, across the universe or whatever. But really cool. They just did six or seven kilometers. Google is launching its own Wi-Fi router. It'd be about $129, and it will have longer range than most routers. It'll also have integrations with smart home features like, you know, lights and if this, then that, and various other, like, you know, air quotes, smart integrations."], [0, "So that'll be pretty cool to see Google entering that market. All right, next one, blog posts. So this is..."], [0, "Original content here, I wrote a post called Authentication's Last Mile. And this is basically about the hard problem of validating when you make a request to something like a bank or in the future, universal demonization, which I'm still trying to finish my book about."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["wifi technology", "emotion detection", "machine learning", "radio frequency", "facial analysis", "surveillance", "privacy", "security", "google assistant", "chat applications", "quantum communication", "photons", "smart home", "wireless routers", "authentication"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778167610.0407312, "duration_sec": 1.7683987617492676, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Right. Yeah. Frankie was like my best friend in the world. You know, he's super middleweight champion of the world. That's who got me deeply into boxing. And so I would always be at his training camps and, you know, I got to train with like Tommy Hearns and all these amazing people like Sugar Ray and all these guys, man."], [1, "Frankie's a great boxing coach."], [0, "Oh, yeah."], [1, "He's one of the most tactical guys I've ever worked with. Like, he analyzes every aspect of your jab. He's pulling in your elbow. He's tightening this. He's moving you here. He's like, he's showing you on all the various basic little tiny details that make all the difference in the world."], [0, "Yeah, man, he was my personal boxing coach a lot of the time. I would train with him, Joe Goossen early on, but Frankie, I mean, we really kind of combined a lot of things. 'Cause I started kind of teaching him things with the jab, like the untelegraphed type of stuff. And he started applying that, and he would, he would bring me into stuff and, you know, have me show people like Sugar Ray, like, oh Mike, explain this jab. And I'm like, what? I'm explaining this to Sugar Ray? This feels ridiculous, right? But, but it was like, but it was like this combination because like, I don't know, I'm very analytical and I love technique. You know, and, and so I would just try to break things down, and my whole thing was always to pressure test things, you know. So if I could develop a tool or a skill and I\u2014 and you can't stop it even if I tell you what's, what's, what I'm doing, then it's a really good technique. Then it's legit."], [1, "The thing about no telegraph at all, it's so much more effective than a harder strike with a telegraph. Oh God, yeah, because it lands. Yeah, but it's so difficult to teach people that because everybody wants a hit hit everybody as hard as they can. Yeah, especially if you have power, your instinct is to fucking load up on everything. I remember I first saw you teaching that to Kimbo Slice. Oh yeah, yeah, you were on a movie set."], [0, "Yeah, yeah, because, um, Kimbo\u2014 oh man, what a great guy."], [1, "What a great guy."], [0, "What a wonderful guy. So that's one thing about fighting, you, you can't hide your nature, you know what I mean? You\u2014 people see who you are, and he was, he was a wonderful human being. But like, but like a lot of people, like almost like street basketball as opposed to, you know, professional, you miss out on certain techniques that you need when you're trying to step up. Right. And so like, well, Kimbo, you know, he would, like a lot of people, he would kind of telegraph. And so when we were shooting the movie, you know, And I basically\u2014 we had a cameraman that did not really know how to shoot stuff, so I just had to do everything on screen. And so I would\u2014 I just wanted to make everything very realistic. And so Kimbo had this rubber knife, and I was like, try your best to touch me with the rubber knife. And so he would try, but before\u2014 but as soon as he would move, there'd be a little bit of an indication that I'd see, and then I throw the punch and it would go really close to him, and I have him react to that. But he was going, \"Wait a minute, how are you hitting me before I can get this knife out?\" And then I told him, you know, I'll show you what that is later. Because, you know, kind of like, not to be real nerdish about it, but like, why are like 50 and 60-year-old trainers meeting people's hands, like a 20-year-old guy's or contender's hands, like this. You see the person with the pad moving just as much as the other guy because there's an indication. There's\u2014 they do this beforehand. They're always kind of flexing and going in reverse before they go forward. So just for over years, I wouldn't do that and I would exploit that, you know. So it's kind of like a cheat code that I'm like, hell, what the hell, what the hell I'm gonna do with it? I'm, I'm an actor. So my thing is, just like yourself, when I see you, you know, George St-Pierre and how we are always in the gym, we're, you know, we're kind of collaborating. We're, you know, we're, we're\u2014 it's about just getting better, not no ego or anything else like that. It's just like, hey man, we're like kind You know, kind of like jamming on technique and getting better."], [1, "Well, especially if you run into someone who has a different style than you. Yeah, because there's always something in different styles that you could take out of it."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["boxing", "fighting", "coaching", "training", "technique", "jab", "footwork", "tactics", "strategy", "sparring", "martial arts", "mixed martial arts", "athletic performance", "skill development", "film acting"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778168341.8710942, "duration_sec": 3.0572762489318848, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[2, "This was my first party. Look at American people, how they doing just in the street, you know? I don't have places to do it."], [1, "Medina flips through the book fondly, as if he's cooing at baby photos."], [2, "And that's the way Algodones start, you know, with that. And like this, you can see all my book is full of parties, you know, to where I go and everything, you know. Well, it's all my life in this book, you know."], [1, "It's clear that Medina put his heart into these parties, into making the town grow the way it did. He convinced his neighbors to cook enough food to feed thousands for free. He hired a mariachi band and converted an old boxing dressing ring into a stage. And the Americans had a great time. Medina made sure the margaritas were strong."], [2, "And they was drunk like crazy, you know. The American customs told me, Dr. Medina, why you give to those guys?"], [0, "Why?"], [2, "They cross right here, they don't want to show the papers, they tell us son of a bitch and go."], [3, "They was very drunk."], [1, "Year after year, Los Salgadones would continue to throw parties like that first one. Americans would return home, talk of the tacos, the town, their teeth. What was once a town of about 750 when Maga\u00f1a first arrived is now crammed with clinics, hotels, restaurants, and pharmacies. Today, young dentists and souvenir vendors from all over Mexico continue to migrate to town in hopes of finding stable work. And the tourists keep coming in droves."], [2, "In winter, it's too much people