{"timestamp": 1778180840.9549606, "duration_sec": 3.191363573074341, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "But Jake, Jake, I got a question for you. I think you can't have an answer. No, I think you do. I know you do. Don't you have\u2014 can't we, can't we get the audio as well?"], [3, "Oh yeah, I was thinking about that."], [2, "Yes, you can be damn certain you get that audio, audio-only version. Um, and I know, uh, the commenters love it when we get into, you know, into the weeds on things and don't talk ball, let's talk football. But, um, we had a major, major crisis with, uh, the software compatibility uploading the audio-only version to Spotify. So another great reminder, you can get your podcast\u2014 we can get the Locked On Lion Squad Show wherever you get your podcast, unless Spotify is having some sort of issue, which I believe has been resolved. So thank you for the, uh, powers that be at Locked On, the fearless leaders. That gave us the statement to read to our listening audience not to worry. Today's episode is still free and available wherever you get your podcast. We're just experiencing some software compatibility issues that we will be taking care of."], [3, "You just got Jake rolling now, Bradford."], [0, "I know I did."], [2, "This is why the bosses are\u2014 yeah, that's why I'm a liability, because I just get one of those moods and you never know what's going to happen. But finally, Detroit Lions NFL Combine, Lucas Oil Stadium, the heart of America, hardworking Midwest Indianapolis. You got to love it this time of year. People say this is when the NFL offseason truly begins. But let's start with Dan Campbell's comments. Any time the boss, the leader of men speaks, we listen. Was part of the podcast, part of the podcast, part of the Combine media availability. And really kind of the biggest thing I took away from Dan Campbell's comments is just still that accountability and acknowledgement that the entire team and organization fell short this year. And, you know, it's, it's not cheesy or clich\u00e9 to say that it really feels like that's fueling the focus of this offseason. And Bradford, I know your, your insight on this, having gone through off-seasons where you know you had more in the tank the previous season. It's got to be, you know, one more example of how that is a fire hopefully igniting each and every member of the Detroit Lions."], [0, "Because one of the things that you do, you know, it starts up top and, you know, with not only with personnel but also with the coaching staff, is, you know, you, you, you get to this point. And how did they get to this point? And what, what you do is you, you strip everything down and then you boil it down to just\u2014 I mean, just down to the nitty-gritty, just down to\u2014 I mean, to the nuts and bolts, let's just say. And, and then you start rebuilding, and then you get to the point where the Lions got over the last couple of years, over the past couple of seasons. And then they, you know, you start getting that number one schedule, you start playing the number one teams, all that kind of stuff. You're looking for that sustainability factor. That did not happen this past season. So you, you go back into the workshop, you strip everything down again. How can we make this better? That's what you do. And I've you know, I've been part of it as a player, I've been part of it as a coach in the NFL, and I've been a part of it in the, in the college level as well. And it's just something that is gratifying whenever you see your body of work play out in public, meaning the games, and you start to have success. Now, the Lions didn't have success this past season Dan has taken accountability for it as well. He should, because he's the head coach and he is the face and basically the voice of the Detroit Lions. And he, you know, it's\u2014 and he's come full, you know, he's come full force with it and he's given himself an F. And, you know, how can we make this better? That is a question that you always ask yourself. You ask yourself every day, even whenever you are having success. Is how can we make this better? What can we do to improve? And it seems like they are taking the necessary steps with, uh, coaching staff moves, with possible personnel moves that could be happening in the, in the near future that they foresee being the right move. Or, you know, whether it's, you know, in the, the court of public opinion, is it the right move? You know, sometimes yes, sometimes no. But whenever the season starts to play out and you see these new pieces that were added start to contribute to the squad in different ways that the old pieces couldn't, then that's where you see\u2014 you start to see the fruit of the labor, uh, starting to bear. So it's, it's, it's an interesting process. To go through. And it's one of these that once you look back on it, you're glad you did it, and you can file it away in your mind once you've gone through it to be like, man, what can we do to sustain so we don't have to do that again? Uh, but unfortunately they have to do\u2014 they're going to have to do it again. Uh, but then, you know, it's It's a, it's a great thing to, to see, and I foresee good things happening with the pieces that are now going to be put in place for the Detroit Lions. And it all starts, you know, it starts now at the Combine, and then you get into free agency, and then you get into the draft, and then you start OTAs, and then, you know, and then training camp, and then the season starts. So it's It's, it's a fun time of year. It's definitely a fun time of year for the talking heads, I can promise you that much."], [3, "That's for damn sure. Excuse my language, but it's true. Like, I feel like you're constantly turning on, yeah, any like ESPN show, whatever, and yeah, the talking heads do love to just talk about whatever they're seeing, whatever, um You know, whatever they're seeing at the Combine. Yeah, you make a really good point with that. But to everything that you just said, Bradford, and watching what Dan Campbell had to say at the Combine, I mean, he is the first one that's able to look himself in the mirror and be totally honest with himself. He constantly takes accountability for, you know, any loss that may happen or a poor season like we had just seen. Um, but I think it also adds an extra fuel to the fire in a sense. Like this, I mean, him describing this season as disappointing, I think even more than losing in the NFC Championship and failing to, you know, move beyond, you know, the Commanders last, you know, the year after that. And then this past year and what it was like, I think not exceeding expectations at all will move this group to do big things. And what stood out to me from Dan's comments was like, look, competition is good for everybody. And Brad Holmes and I need to bring in other guys that make other guys uncomfortable because competition is good for everyone. And he said, it was really good for me as a player. I know it'll be good for this team. And so we need to get back to that. Like, that is our job to set these guys up for success. But sometimes you need a little bit of pushing to get back there. And I think they're going to do the best job they can this offseason to bring in some of those pieces. To push this group. So he's just as motivated as anyone else. But when you\u2014 you could just hear in his voice at the Combine too that like he is ready to get back to who this team is and just the DNA that they're built on, because this past year just didn't reflect that for multiple reasons. But, um, I like what he had to say. I like that he's always honest. He doesn't hide, he doesn't deflect. That's what you can appreciate about a coach like that."], [2, "For sure, that, that competitive nature certainly at the forefront of, of the head coach and Brad Holmes and the entire organization on down. But we're getting some awesome comments. We've got John Smith's lined up there in the queue. We'll get to that question about, uh, who the Lions might be eyeing and who we\u2014 you know, speaking of, excuse me, of talking heads, you know, we got to give our draft picks. So obviously, you know, we wouldn't pass up such an wonderful opportunity to do that. We'll do that, and then we will talk, uh, Taylor Decker return season, revenge season, whatever we want to call it. That's coming up next on a live Locked On Lions Squad Show. Hey, today's episode of the Locked On Lions Squad Show is brought to you by FanDuel. Have you ever wanted to experience the NBA Finals live and in person? Of course you have. Well, FanDuel is giving you that chance. Turn that dream into a reality with their NBA sweepstakes. Here's how it works: you use your profit boost on any NBA future and you automatically are entered for a shot to win an NBA Finals trip for two. That's right, flights, tickets, the whole shebang, the whole experience. And you know what, if you're already looking at the board and thinking about which team can make a deep run, this is the perfect time to lock it in. And you know me, I love futures this time of year, whether it's picking a team to win the championship or grab a conference title. Here's a spoiler alert: Detroit Pistons all the way. Best team in the East, best team in the league, it does not matter. And you know what? It adds that extra layer of excitement to every game down the stretch. So visit Fanduel.com to get started. Use your profits boost on an NBA future and get entered for a chance to win a trip to the NBA Finals. Play your game with FanDuel, the official sports betting partner of the NBA."], [3, "Having the right people in your corner for life's biggest milestones makes all the difference."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Sports"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["nfl", "detroit lions", "football", "coaching", "accountability", "team culture", "competition", "offseason", "draft", "free agency", "nfl combine", "podcast", "spotify", "sports betting", "nba finals"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778182494.8444498, "duration_sec": 6.034438133239746, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "SEATTLE CITY COUNCIL\n600 Fourth Ave. 2nd Floor\nSeattle, WA 98104\nJournal of the Proceedings of the Seattle City Council\nTuesday, April 21, 2026\n2:00 PM\nCouncil Chamber, City Hall\n600 4th Avenue\nSeattle, WA 98104\nCity Council\nJoy Hollingsworth, Council President\nDionne Foster, Member\nDebora Juarez, Member\nRobert Kettle, Member\nEddie Lin, Member\nAlexis Mercedes Rinck, Member\nMaritza Rivera, Member\nRob Saka, Member\nDan Strauss, Member\nChair Info: 206-684-8803; Joy.Hollingsworth@seattle.gov\n\nCity Council Meeting Minutes April 21, 2026\nA. CALL TO ORDER\nThe City Council of The City of Seattle met in the Council Chamber in City\nHall in Seattle, Washington, on April 21, 2026, pursuant to the provisions of\nthe City Charter. The meeting was called to order at 2:03 p.m., with Council\nPresident Hollingsworth presiding.\nB. ROLL CALL\nPresent: 8 - Foster, Hollingsworth, Juarez, Kettle, Lin, Rinck, Rivera, Saka\nExcused: 1 - Strauss\nC. PRESENTATIONS\nThere were none.\nD. PUBLIC COMMENT\nThe following indi"]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["seattle city council", "joy hollingsworth", "dionne foster", "debora juarez", "robert kettle", "eddie lin", "alexis mercedes rinck", "maritza rivera", "rob saka", "dan strauss", "seattle city hall", "600 4th avenue seattle wa 98104", "seattle city charter"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778182554.2482634, "duration_sec": 1.7916483879089355, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Airflow and the CubeRay and basically like allowing you to run Ray on top of like any type of like hardware cluster management solution. So we really like try to view Ray as this kind of like, like if people, I don't know if I'm dating myself, but you know, in the internet model, there's like the narrow waste, right? Which is like TCP IP. So we view Ray as kind of like the narrow waste of the like AI or like distributed computing systems."], [0, "One more thing. I think we've got time to talk just a little bit about the business model. So over on Ray.io, I can see that I can go to like GitHub or go to the docs, but also you've got AnyScale, which lets you basically is the infrastructure behind running Ray, right? Is that this sort of the business side of Ray? AnyScale is a company, but also a product. So for example, like Ray is like a software library that you can run, but there is a lot of"], [0, "If you're sort of deploying Rape or you're like an internal platform for a company, like there's still a lot of other bells and whistles that you'll sort of want. So for example, like being able to have a fast interactive development, being able to optimize like the time it takes for the workloads to start up, having great observability and debuggability. And yeah,"], [0, "being able to sort of like share resources across different teams within like across different RAI jobs and then also be able to optimize your RAI workloads. So these are all like features and capabilities that you get with any scale. And yeah, and then also like, you know, support, being able to sort of deploy and manage and upstream fixes to RAI that sort of help your enterprise like in your company achieve the goals and needs for your machine learning platform. That's like a lot of stuff that we do."], [0, "You know, I think this is one of the core ways that people are making open source stuff their business, right? Like we built you a great library, but there's this whole operational side of it that you maybe either don't want to do or you don't have a bunch of servers or whatever. And we'll just, for a price, we'll just take care of that, right?"], [0, "There's a couple of ways that you can go. So one thing I want to say is that having a successful company behind Ray is critical for its health. There's no way that we could have built as many of the libraries and funded as many of the ecosystem integrations and just built something with as big of a scope as Ray if we didn't have a company backing it, paying as many people to work on it as were."], [0, "And yeah, I think there's a few different ways you can go about this kind of open source monetization thing. Any scale model is largely this managed infrastructure and the hard parts around it. There's some people that also kind of go for the more support expertise model. I think that could work if you really want to stay small."], [0, "Like if you have a smaller open source project, it's just a couple of people. And like, you know, you're trying to make enough money to survive and keep working on that project. Then honestly, I think that's the easier route than trying to build a whole managed product because it's, it's not easy. It's kind of a, kind of just a consulting story. This, this other side you're talking is like, I, I will be your X open source project X consultant. And guess what? I created it. So I'm, who else is going to be better? You know what I mean?"], [0, "That's very real. I would recommend a lot of open source people consider that, even if it's just the start of something, because that's the way that you really engage with people and understand their problems and understand where the business value is. 100%. Let me ask you one more tech-oriented question before we call it. What about deployment?"], [0, "I have 10 servers in my cluster. I changed one line in my code and I want to try it now. Now what? How hard is it to get it to update everywhere? So that is something that we, that I personally spent a lot of time working on. I think that Ray actually has a very good story for it. So there's like, there's kind of a tiered approach. So it sort of depends. Like obviously if you're changing, like if you need a different, like CUDA version or something, then that will require you to basically like redeploy the cluster."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["distributed computing", "ray", "machine learning", "cloud infrastructure", "cluster management", "deployment", "open source", "business model", "managed service", "enterprise software", "observability", "debugging", "scalability", "devops", "consulting"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
