 Imperative of Bridge Financing in 2025 The architecture of real estate finance has fundamentally shifted."], [2, "Bridge and Construction Lending | Axos Bank\nCommercial Real Estate Specialty Bridge and Construction Lending We specialize in providing bridge and construction loan options to owners, investors, and developers who need time to make improvements, find a new tenant, or sell a property. Our extensive experience and streamlined processing help ensure fast and smooth closings."], [3, "Have Cap Rates Peaked? - Commercial Property Executive\nAug 6, 2024 \u00b7 Doug Ressler, manager of business intelligence at Yardi Matrix, told Commercial Property Executive that it appears that cap rates have indeed peaked but ongoing uncertainty is expected to delay sales volume recovery until 2025 . \u201cDuring the first half of 2024 , cap rates held steady despite fluctuations in Treasury yields,\u201d Ressler said."], [4, "U.S. Cap Rate Survey H2 2024 - CBRE\nFeb 12, 2025 \u00b7 More than 200 CBRE real estate professionals completed the H2 2024 CRS with their real -time market estimates between November and December. Given the rapidly changing macro environment, survey results may not reflect recent exogenous events or current market conditions. Readers should view all cap rate estimates within this context."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": null, "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["construction loans", "bridge loans", "project financing", "real estate finance", "property development", "commercial real estate", "interest rates", "investment returns", "property valuation", "market trends", "transaction data", "financing", "commercial lending", "residential construction", "multifamily housing", "single family homes", "cap rates", "treasury yields", "sales volume", "market uncertainty"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778183494.023387, "duration_sec": 2.078922986984253, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Even like telling that story because it sounds like bullshit. It really happened that way because then the guard\u2014 I talked to the guard. I'm like, hey, when did the lights go out? Oh, it shook and the lights went out. I'm like, I'm watching that happen."], [1, "So you felt it happen before it happened."], [0, "Some kind of weird way."], [1, "Well, I bet humans have that. Animals definitely have that. They talk about Thailand. How they had that tsunami and all the animals ran up to the highest point of the island. They all just took off. It was like they just knew instinctively."], [0, "I don't know. Nothing like that has ever happened afterwards. But I gotta say, there's been\u2014 I've been lucky over the years."], [1, "Yeah, but you're a dude who's tuned in. You're tuned into your body. You're tuned into your environment. You're not gonna get caught slipping. Like, you probably felt something and your spidey sense went off."], [0, "Yeah, I kind of have been like that growing up. Like, I've been, you know, I've been on my own since I was 14. Been through crazy shit that you normally would see on movies."], [1, "And that's the type of shit that gives you those kind of instincts."], [0, "But yeah, and I was always the one that said, \"Hey, let's leave. Let's get out of here.\" And then, \"Hey, man, there was a shootout that just happened right after you left.\" Or I could detect, like, The predators, you know what I mean?"], [1, "Mm-hmm."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["intuition", "instinct", "sixth sense", "danger", "survival", "awareness", "trauma", "self protection", "street life", "violence", "predators", "animals", "natural disasters", "tsunami", "perception"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778183632.7084477, "duration_sec": 1.6457548141479492, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "In our case, our AI SRE was meant to be agentic from the start. What we didn't know when we started, when we thought about building an AI SRE, we didn't know that this was before reasoning models were introduced. And we made a bet on reasoning models, but we didn't know that they will"], [0, "became reality so quickly. So we started looking into that space by the end of 2024. And our first attempt was like, how can we simulate a human that essentially opens a bunch of tabs and looks at a bunch of graphs and dashboards and looks at logs. And that was our first idea. So let's have an AI use a browser and then make screenshots of images."], [0, "which is like by image, I mean, could be a list of logs, could be a dashboard, and then try to understand and create a whole picture of the situation. So yeah, to answer your question, yes, we wanted it to be agentic, but we had a different approach in the first place. So can you tell me a little bit about the evolution?"], [0, "Yeah, it started as an idea and the idea, like the implementation, we never pursued that exact implementation. These were ideas in the beginning. And at one point, I think this was in April, where first reasoning models were launched and then something else happened. The model context protocol took off, right? So initially we thought, okay, when there is an incident,"], [0, "we have all the data, right? We do know where to go because when we receive an alert from a system, the responder would look at the incoming, the source links and would just go to the source of the alert and then would access its systems. And this is what we try to initially mimic, having an alert and then essentially doing all the steps by going to the source systems. But we once, reasoning models,"], [0, "were a reality. And once MCP, the MCP protocol was launched and MCP was something that took off really quickly. So it was all over the place and people were building their own MCP servers, which doesn't mean that it was good from the beginning. And today we were even considering different approaches than using the MCP protocol. For those who are not familiar with the model context protocol, it's essentially a protocol that lets agents access external systems to"], [0, "using a standard that was initially published by Anthropic and was picked up and supported by all the frontier labs, including Google, including OpenAI. Once we had those two ingredients, model context protocol, and we had reasoning models, we started building an agent. What we didn't know back then, you were asking about evolution. So first we started, although we had reasoning models, we wanted to be very prescriptive about that particular problem. We literally had a prompt, the system instructions that were more than a thousand lines."], [0, "We would tell, okay, this is the usual path that leads to success. Please follow this. Here are a bunch of rules. And then we did a bunch of iterations. And what we have today is different than what we had a year ago. And the space is, as probably many know, moving very fast. And we're trying to somewhat predict where models are going and trying to benefit from model capabilities from the start."], [0, "So as you point out, this is evident in many talks. There was recently something from the OpenAI platform engineering lead. I'll put a link to that in the show notes, where he also points out that models are improving so fast that any scaffolding that you put in, any framework that you put in becomes outdated pretty quickly. Seems like that has been your experience so far as well."], [0, "A hundred percent. And for us as engineers, we sometimes tend to over-engineer, right? When we look to solve a problem. And I think this happens all the time, right? And what we end up doing is removing things and simplifying things after we've built something. But the difference with AI is it's moving so fast. For example, one of the first AI use cases that we introduced was an intelligent alert grouping that's based on semantics instead of text, right?"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["artificial intelligence", "site reliability engineering", "incident response", "agent systems", "reasoning models", "automation", "monitoring", "logs", "dashboards", "alerting", "alert grouping", "prompt engineering", "model context protocol", "platform engineering", "software engineering"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778184016.6852052, "duration_sec": 1.8248462677001953, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "That those younger entrepreneurs just couldn't take a chance on accessing credit and they weren't credit worthy because of that indebtedness. And so exactly to your point, all of these ideas are not coming to market, are not being realized in our communities because you have that stranglehold and just this wall that's preventing them from moving forward."], [0, "Yeah, for your fund, how do you get the word out that your fund exists, that you're open for business and that you're looking for people to give money to? Because I imagine that when the system is stacked so much against women, against people of color, that a lot of them who have business ideas might have kind of given up on maybe looking for capital a while ago or something. Totally. Yeah, you're spot on. The fund was born out of the lived experience of"], [0, "Me and then three other women entrepreneurs in Oregon, we'd had a really hard time accessing capital to grow our own companies. And so we went out into the ecosystem and we were told, well, if you don't feel like there are enough women getting funded, you have to start your own fund. We were like, okay."], [0, "More work for me. I know. Yeah. Thank you. It was a huge gift, though. It was it's been one of the most profound experiences of my life in terms of learning all of these intricacies of how to go about doing it. And then all of the barriers in the way I feel so privileged. The group of women that started this, you know, we have extraordinary privilege and education to be able to push this forward."], [0, "I cannot imagine someone else trying to do this. But in answer to your question, we started an education program called Accelerate. And the Accelerator is a program for Oregon women entrepreneurs where they go through a three-month curriculum to get them capital ready so that when they're ready to apply for that loan product from..."], [0, "in partnership with Colorado Lending Source. They have everything that they need. And what we're seeing is that women have a lot of questions. To your point, if you have been on the venture circuit for the last year and you've been rejected by 100 investors, your confidence is super low. If you've been focusing on making pitch decks, that's really different from creating financial forecasts and profit and loss statements."], [0, "If you have never really had to realistically interrogate what's possible with your business, it requires a different skill set to develop a marketing strategy and sales strategy. And so our capital coach at the end of that program prepares women to be loan ready."], [0, "And the goal of that program is really for as many women as possible to be profitable and sustainable, bankable or investable. And so our capital coaches are helping to prepare them to make it possible. But that is not an algorithm. It's not a traditional bank. It's really people and coaches taking a deep, sincere interest in the growth of their business."], [0, "And it sounds like a different conversation around growth and sustainability and profit is also needed, right? Because it's like to make a true alternative to, let's say, the current venture capital system, you also have to examine the whole incentive structure and what these founders are prodded to do in the name of satisfying their investors, right? Yeah."], [0, "Totally. Yeah. And that's where the Zebras Unite movement comes in. And Jason was a guest on our podcast called Zebra Cast. So I encourage all of you to listen to that. The Zebras Unite movement is an international movement of founders that are exactly advocating for what you're talking about, which is we have to create new capital systems, corporate structures, community development."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["entrepreneurship", "small business", "access to capital", "lending", "credit", "venture capital", "women entrepreneurs", "racial equity", "financial literacy", "business coaching", "accelerator programs", "economic development", "investment readiness", "community development", "alternative finance"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778184555.4984033, "duration_sec": 2.631046772003174, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah, but my best experience is is with like, I got a chance to train against champions at their place, you know, when they're at their best. And it's not an ego thing. It's just like, I love to be able to test myself. And I mean, because I'm my biggest competition. And so that whole thing about just what the bow means to me is like, thank you for\u2014 making me better, right, by providing me an obstacle. And the higher the\u2014 you know, the better the person, the better I can become. 100%."], [1, "And so I loved it."], [0, "So I've, you know, for years I'm in there with Gokhan Saki and, you know, Maurice Smiths, and, you know, who\u2014 you know, you name it. I\u2014 there's\u2014 I've gotten\u2014 I consider myself one of the luckiest one of the greatest martial artists on the planet because I get to train with so many people. Sometimes at my house, and I've got all these former champions training. Rampage, when he was champion, I'd go to his place. And honestly, the things I brag about is when I get humbled. Because that's when I learn something. My philosophy is I love to be wrong because every time I'm wrong, I learn something. Something. Absolutely. And so, like, some of the best times for me is like, I know when I was, you know, Michael Bisping was getting ready for, to fight Georges St-Pierre, and we were in Thailand. I was like, yeah, you know, let's, let's like mix it up."], [1, "What were you doing in Thailand?"], [0, "Oh, we were doing a movie out there. Oh, wow. But he had to train. He was get