changes now. You pick. Glasses half full, glasses half empty. Oh, one more good news. I touched on this earlier. I wrote about it today at CowboysRoundtable.com."], [0, "I am suggesting that in an effort to get the George Pickens camp, featuring agent David Mulugeta, to realize how much the Cowboys want to play nice there, that they overpaid and brought in PJ Locke. The backup safety from Denver gave him $5 million. Now, he's a Christian Parker. I'm sorry. Yeah, he's a Christian Parker guy. That's good. I say that that is an olive branch. Abe Shepard, how about getting Fred Warner? I'm in."], [0, "That's tier one. Got to trade for him. There is obviously a vacancy at linebacker. I'd be all in on that. A peace offering, maybe, in the George Pickens thing. That counts as good news. Item nine. Are you familiar with Steve Tisch? Billionaire, co-owner of the Giants."], [0, "He's in the Epstein files. They enjoyed supplying each other with chicks. He's in trouble, or should be. The NFL hadn't said a word. Today comes the story"], [0, "Steve Tisch is asking the NFL, can I just transfer my stake in the team? And I think he owns 43% of the Giants. So that's a $10 billion franchise, right? I'm not very good at math. That's $4 billion. Can I just transfer my ownership, the stakes to my kids and put in a trust? He's going to get away with that."], [0, "Instead of getting booted out of the league, he's going to slide to the side. And I don't know about you and your relationship with your kids, but if I give $4 billion to Nate and Tony, I still kind of have it. Right? I still kind of have it. Alan P.,"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["nfl", "dallas cowboys", "player contracts", "trade rumors", "sports journalism", "roster moves", "free agency", "salary cap", "linebackers", "team management", "ownership", "scandal", "new york giants", "minnesota vikings", "player agents"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778187527.2835016, "duration_sec": 2.1099398136138916, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "has been Corey's best friend since ninth grade. She testified about being at the Richens' home on March 6th of 2022, the morning after Eric's body was found. Corey was trying to get into Eric's safe to find the will. Eric's sister told Corey the house was no longer hers. A locksmith was called. Amy Richens ran into the garage and physically stopped him. Corey was shaking, crying. According to another witness, she had pure frustration and rage. She collapsed. She was hysterical. Chelsea was there for all of it."], [0, "What Chelsea may not have fully understood at the time was that her relationship with Corey was actually costing her. She testified that Corey had offered her a home to buy when she was flipping. Chelsea, a restaurant manager who had saved her tips for years, wired Corey $45,000 as a down payment, her life savings. She then paid $2,900 a month in mortgage payments."], [0, "but the deed was never properly recorded with the county. When Corey was arrested, Chelsea discovered that an unauthorized loan had been taken out on the property without her knowledge. She was evicted in September of 24. The house had been handed over to someone else to cover Corey's debts. The woman standing by her best friend's side in the hours after Eric died had already been quietly cleaned out by her."], [0, "This is what Corey is capable of. If you don't think she's capable of murder, well, then I'm sorry. There's a lot in life that's escaped you."], [0, "But Chelsea's testimony cuts deepest not in what happened to her house, but in what she said in those text threads. In May of 22, Corey wrote to Chelsea that investigators had found nothing on either of them, not nothing on me, either of us. Two women in the text exchange, and Corey is already framing it as a shared situation. One where Chelsea apparently needed to be reassured she wasn't in legal jeopardy either."], [0, "Whether Chelsea understood the weight of what was being said to her is an open question. The jury has it now either way. Then a month later, in June of 22, in an exchange, Chelsea asked Corey, what would you have to put in a sandwich for him to break out in hives? Two best friends in a text thread discussing what could have been an Eric Richen's food to make him sick."], [0, "Corey's response? She didn't make the sandwiches. That's it. No shock. No denial of the premise. Just deflection."], [0, "Whether Chelsea was trying to help Corey think through her defense or genuinely puzzled about what happened, the jury now has that exchange sitting in front of them. And the answer Corey chose to give. And then there's this. On June 6th, 22, Corey texted Chelsea that she was running to get the death certificate before Eric's sister could. Minutes later, she sent Chelsea a photo of it, the document confirming fentanyl, and writes that she thinks it's finally over."], [0, "And that she's relieved, not shattered, not devastated, she's relieved. There's no world in which a prosecutor leaves that word alone, and Brad Bloodworth didn't. And then there are the calls to Bryce Kunson, Eric's best friend. Recorded March 19th to 22, just weeks after the funeral, Corey on the call is"], [0, "I don't care about money. It's not a big effing deal to me. Eric has three million in life insurance money. I want it all to go to the trust for my kids. Then, within minutes of saying she doesn't care about money, she's listing her properties, 12 of them, describing what she's owed, explaining the $250,000 home equity loan she took without Eric's knowledge, and insisting on keeping the house."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["true crime", "murder investigation", "courtroom testimony", "trial evidence", "text messages", "financial fraud", "real estate", "property deed", "mortgage payments", "life insurance", "inheritance", "family dispute", "fentanyl overdose", "trust fund", "friendship betrayal"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778187796.1269765, "duration_sec": 1.9749791622161865, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "with very minimal tech, no app, no analytics, no coaching. And then on the other end of the spectrum was Tonal, which is also a great product, but $4,000 upfront plus a subscription, it can be prohibitively expensive for a lot of people. And so we just started looking at dumbbells more and more. The more we looked into it, even on Statista, we started to see that dumbbells are actually"], [0, "the largest TAM of any fitness modality. You could argue that they're the most popular, most used, most familiar, and also arguably the most iconic piece of equipment today and historically. And so we thought, hey, what if we bring some of Brian's robotics technology into here and make dumbbells better?"], [0, "No, that's great. I was also really, you know, when we were first chatting, struck by, you know, the fact that, you know, again, some of these larger connective fitness platforms are great, but require, you know, quite a bit of real estate when it comes to sort of, you know, how to incorporate it into your, you know, at-home life. And I know something I really appreciate about Kabato is just sort of how versatile it could be. You know, it could really be plugged into a small New York or SF apartment pretty easily. So, yeah, I see all the benefits, of course. Yeah, exactly."], [0, "Exactly. I mean, just to touch on that quickly too, that was actually one of the biggest pain points that we"], [0, "We ran surveys in the early days, asking people a variety of questions across what's the maximum weight you'd want per dumbbell? What's your biggest pain points in at-home fitness today, et cetera, et cetera. And on that latter question, the biggest pain points for people were, I can't afford what's currently on the market. A lot of the products across bikes, rowers, mirrors, machines were several thousand dollars plus a subscription. And then the second pain point that they mentioned was, I don't have"], [0, "enough space in my small home or apartment for this equipment. And so that's where we felt Kabata at our price point at under two by two square feet of space, combining 12 sets of dumbbells into one with all the tech and features that we pack into this thing, basically as a personal trainer and coach at your fingertips, we felt that was pretty compelling. Yeah."], [0, "Absolutely. And that's really great. I want to shift gears a little bit because I know you're coming off of a pretty big announcement, which is the company's seed fundraise, which we were thrilled to be a part of. We'd love to share any of the specifics you feel comfortable and really what you're hoping to accomplish as part of this raise."], [0, "Yeah. Yeah. So we raised it early 2023 with Sean Carolyn at Menlo Ventures leading our round. It was a $5 million seed round. Sean Carolyn actually just made the Midas list again about a couple of weeks ago, which was timed almost perfectly with our announcement of the round, which we kind of delayed for about 18 months deliberately. But yeah, one of the top 100 investors globally, early investor in Uber, Roku, Chime, Siri, Jump Bikes, just a plethora of really successful"], [0, "companies. So really just great conviction, strong conviction to have someone of his caliber believe in us. And it's crazy because even kind of come in full circle with all this, I used to work in venture capital myself and I always kind of looked up to Sean sort of as an outsider looking in, seeing his investment, seeing his success and always wanting to work with him. And so it's pretty crazy now that he actually led our seat round and is like at our board meetings. Wow. Yeah. No, what a full circle moment. That's great."], [0, "As you think about the horizon a bit, can you paint us a picture of what you're marching towards as part of the round?"]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["fitness", "home workouts", "strength training", "dumbbells", "exercise equipment", "connected fitness", "robotics", "wearable technology", "product design", "affordability", "small spaces", "market research", "startup funding", "seed round", "venture capital"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
