{"timestamp": 1778189214.2504191, "duration_sec": 1.3205981254577637, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "But Google has told us they've unveiled the secret sauce that, yes, they will render your React application and have a look at that code, and they will judge your website accordingly based on that. So we don't have to worry so much. So that's like one argument away from having to do server-side rendering. Server-side rendering is..."], [0, "It adds a lot of complexity to your application, because now in your JavaScript code, when you're writing your application, typically you might do things like, I want to access a property of the window object, which only exists in the browser. It doesn't exist on the server. There's no window object, because there's no browser on the server. When you're rendering, when you're in server-side rendering, you're doing it inside of Node.js. There is no browser."], [0, "So typically you have to have a lot of if-else statements in your code base to say like, hey, if I'm on the client and then I can access window and do this thing, else if I'm on the server, then I need to access the file system and do this thing to get the piece of data that I want."], [0, "So it ends up making your application kind of messy. And so I should touch on the third thing, which is pre-rendering or static rendering. And that's when you take every permutation of every piece of state that your application could come up with on the backend and you render it before any requests show up on your web server at all. And then when a request shows up to your web server, you say, oh, you're looking for this"], [0, "state of an application and you're asking for it based on a route that you've chosen. And I already have that state of the application rendered. I've saved it to a static HTML file over here. I'm just going to send that back to you. And so it solves the problem of client-side rendering in that you're not going to get a blank white screen at the very beginning and then you have to download some JavaScript. You're going to get some actual content and..."], [0, "It solves one of the problems of server side rendering, which is that rendering on the server is actually expensive. Rendering on the client is expensive. Rendering on the server, just rendering in general is kind of an expensive operation. So rendering on a server is expensive. And I have a pretty good horror story of how it actually took down some web servers at one point."], [0, "Let's hear that, although I want to be clear of these three approaches, client-side, server-side, and I guess static, which is Next.js enabling? Can you do all three with React alone, or which is Next.js providing, I guess?"], [0, "Next.js gives you the ability to do all three. It helps make the server-side rendering easier. It removes some of the boilerplate stuff for you. It can do all the static rendering for you, and it depends on how you set it up. My preference is still client-side rendering in most cases, and then pre-rendering for some other cases. I'm not a huge proponent of server-side rendering."], [0, "And that's going to surprise a lot of people because Next.js sells server-side rendering really hard. You know, we do this for you and it gives you some benefits and there are some benefits there. But some of the problems with server-side rendering is your application is not actually functional and useful in the browser until you've downloaded that expensive piece of JavaScript and you've hydrated your application."], [0, "So yes, you will get that first paint really quickly and you'll see some content on the screen, but nothing's going to work. None of your buttons are going to work. Your site's not interactive until you've downloaded the JavaScript bundle and hydrated that application. So there's a moment of frustration for the user that happens there when you're staring at something, but it's not working. And so maybe you solve that with loading screens and whatnot."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["react", "nextjs", "javascript", "web development", "server side rendering", "client side rendering", "static rendering", "prerendering", "seo", "performance", "hydration", "nodejs", "user experience", "web servers", "application architecture"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
