{"timestamp": 1778191488.2816265, "duration_sec": 1.792935848236084, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And then the final quality that you mentioned in the book, which is somewhat surprising to me because it's so detailed, is threat safety. So readability is like this super generic concept that you can apply to almost everything within a project. Readability is not even limited to code. It also holds for documentation and APIs, as we talked about."], [0, "But threat safety is so specific. And I would say it's somewhat related also to reliability. So why did you decide to make threat safety its own quality rather than integrated it into reliability?"], [0, "Right. Yeah, you're absolutely right. So thread safety stands out as a little different than the others. And first of all, as you say, at first sight, at least, it's more specific than the others."], [0, "Like up until a few years ago, it would have been considered really a very specific quality. But these days, as you know, more and more software is inherently concurrent. And that's because of hardware development where every machine is concurrent these days. It's actually parallel these days."], [0, "And computers are not getting much faster in a single core point of view. So more and more software is becoming concurrent. So I think threat safety is going to become more and more important and should be considered almost as important as those other qualities. But of course, it's a part of reliability."], [0, "So thread safety is about having your class able to be used by several threads at the same time without data races, which means without unexpected behaviors, without getting into an inconsistent state."], [0, "And also, I have to admit another reason why I put it there is because it fits very well with my recurring example. We haven't talked about it. I'll just say a few words. The recurring example is a simple, I hope, very intuitive water container example. You have a system of water containers. You can pour water into one container, and you can connect different containers with the so-called pipes."], [0, "with imaginary pipes. And when you pour water into one container, this water that you pour should disperse equally among all containers that are connected to this one, among all containers that you have previously connected to this one with pipes."], [0, "And that, I think, turns out to be a very interesting example. And it's very tricky to make it thread safe. So I have to admit another reason why I chose this particular quality, besides the fact that it's becoming more and more important every day, is"], [0, "that it fits very well with this example because it's very tricky to make this water container class thread safe. And it allows me to talk about different synchronization techniques, including lock-free synchronization, which is a pretty advanced topic. So the final thing I want to talk about is, and this is people that listen to my episodes, often they know this topic always comes up and that's the topic of different programming languages."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["software engineering", "code quality", "readability", "documentation", "api design", "reliability", "thread safety", "concurrency", "parallel computing", "synchronization", "data races", "lock free programming", "programming languages", "hardware performance", "multithreading"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778191581.1148846, "duration_sec": 2.142524003982544, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[3, "They really\u2014 they really only come when they have to."], [1, "Yeah."], [3, "The reason they don't like it is because of the immigration, the way they treat them when they're crossing. They always get harassed. Yeah. When they're going back to America, they get harassed. So they get tired of that."], [1, "Los Algodones has long been set up for the benefit of Americans, and the journey Americans take to cross into Molar City has always been a simple one. But of course, the journey in the opposite direction is much more complicated. Can I ask you a question that might sound\u2014 I don't know, I don't know how you'll take it, but I'll ask it anyway. You can tell me how you react to it. I've heard from Americans and Canadians, like people describing what it's like to go down to Los Algodones, and people describe just walking freely through the border, like just 5 minutes, no need to even show a passport. And I, I'm just curious what that's like for you on your end, knowing that you were kicked out, and yet part of your job is to interact daily with I don't know, hundreds of people who just walk so freely across this border, you know?"], [3, "The way I see it is like this. Since I am from the border and we lived in America, we went to school in America, what happens is that when I see people coming from America and I see myself that I can't go back, which is like literally Right there, I can see my mom's house from right here."], [1, "Oh, wow."], [3, "That's the hardest thing to see. I see my house. I got the Colorado River, and I can literally see the water tank at the reservation, and then I can see all the way to the Yuma hospital on 24th Street, and then I kind of know more or less where my mom's house is over here. If I had a drone, I could fly it over there and and look at and go, \"Ouch.\"."], [1, "Yeah."], [3, "That's how close I am. That's not easy. It's very hard for me."], [1, "It's 5:00 AM in Algodones. The sky is still dark, not quite sunrise, and Alberto is shuffling around the house, getting ready for for work. The streets are mostly quiet at this hour, just the sound of other promoters and vendors setting up, setting the stage for Molar City. By 6:00 AM, he's at his post. The border opens and the Americans descend. They walk in the shadow of the 30-foot wall that divides them. Crossing the very threshold Alberto cannot."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["immigration", "border crossing", "deportation", "travel restrictions", "discrimination", "identity", "family separation", "mexico", "united states", "border town", "customs enforcement", "social inequality", "indigenous community", "daily work", "wall"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778191613.6805248, "duration_sec": 1.7440152168273926, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[2, "This has been your Daily Brief in AI."], [1, "To read more about these stories, follow."], [0, "The links in the episode bio."], [1, "You can also subscribe to these updates."], [0, "Via email at www.brief.news. For more daily podcasts about the topics."], [4, "You love, visit."], [3, "Brief.News/Podcasts. Tune in tomorrow. We'll be back with everything you need to know."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["artificial intelligence", "daily news", "podcast", "email subscription", "newsletter", "updates", "technology", "media", "online links", "episode bio", "brief summary", "information", "news stories", "audio content", "website"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778191794.2675228, "duration_sec": 1.541154384613037, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Welcome to a new episode of the Front End Happy Hour podcast. An important part of shipping features to production is having solid automated tests. In this episode, we are joined by Janicki and Benoit to talk with us about automation at scale. Janicki and Benoit, can you give brief introductions of who you are, what you do, and what your favorite happy hour beverage is?"], [0, "Hi, my name is Janaki Ramachandran, and I lead a team which is doing automation infrastructure for both client devices and for some of the server components. And my favorite happy hour drink is probably a red wine, sometimes beer. Hi, and thank you, Ryan, for receiving us. I'm Benoit Fontaine. I'm a senior software engineer at Netflix. I've been at Netflix for over seven years now."], [0, "under Janaki, and I've been working on test and device automation, building the infrastructure for testing. As Janaki mentioned, it's mainly in the client teams, which is the Netflix application that you have on TV, mobile, browsers. But there is also more use over time in the server side as well."], [0, "For drinks, I am French, so I love wine. So a red wine is also great. But when I need something more cool, chill, I definitely like beer, and Belgian beer in particular, like triples. Right on. You guys, both good choices on the happy hour beverage. That's great. And Benoit, you and I probably started at Netflix around the same time, because I think I'm just over seven years now, too. So that's kind of cool. Yeah, 2015."], [0, "Oh yeah. Yeah. Same here. It was like April, 2015 for me. I don't know what time, which month you were, but March. Yeah. Oh yes. Very close. I love it. That's really cool."], [0, "All right, well, let's give introductions of today's panelists. I'm the only panelist today. So I'm Ryan Burgess. I'm a software engineering manager at Netflix. In each episode of the Friday Unhappy Hour podcast, we like to choose a keyword that if it's mentioned at all in the episode, we will all take a drink. Now, what did we decide today's keyword is? Release. Yeah, we try to avoid test and automation."], [0, "Yeah. So releases is probably going to come up, but maybe not as much as tests and automation. So that should be good. All right. I thought it was a good way to level set as we dive in to the topic. It's like, what is automation? Like, I think automation can mean a lot of things, but like, what is automation around testing? Yeah. So test automation is, is not something to, to be,"], [0, "to consider as a binary thing, like you have automation or you don't, it has a right range of domain. It's a gradient of automation. So you can have full automation or partial automation or no automation at all. So usually what it involves is it starts from cataloging tests, meaning you have a set of tests that you know you have that you could run. So you need to know, catalog them."], [0, "then at some point you need to choose select and automate the selection of the tests you want to run in certain condition. And then when it's time to run them after selecting them, there's automation around executing them in an automated manner. Then you want the reporting to some system to be automated. You don't want people to necessarily do spreadsheets by hand when they report."], [0, "And there is then, I would say, action that happens around the test. So test themselves, you know, they do their own validation. But you might want to trigger actions in an automated fashion based on what happened with the test. So first, you could maybe have some statistics, you know, about what happened in the test that you want to calculate at the end. Or you might want to trigger action like notification, you know, this test failed, I need to notify this."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["software testing", "test automation", "automation infrastructure", "continuous integration", "continuous delivery", "release management", "quality assurance", "testing strategy", "test execution", "test reporting", "device testing", "client applications", "server components", "software engineering", "scaling systems"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778191849.3582032, "duration_sec": 1.4865303039550781, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[2, "It was very hard, you know. We seen the police and comes there. He says\u2014and Mariana says\u2014your work is not good. You have to give the money back to the lady."], [1, "Police in Los Algodones exist to protect the industry and to make tourists feel safe. There's an old promotional video for Molar City still up on YouTube. It's from 12 years ago, and it's less than a minute long. In the clip, a police officer does a salsa twirl with an elderly white woman. The video is titled \"All Tourists Are Safe and Fun in Los Lagodones.\"."], [4, "There's parties, there's fun, there's bars, there's cheap food and drinks, there's dental care, there's a ton of pharmaceuticals. And the police are, are watching. If there's any sense of like any trouble brewing, police will be very much on it."], [1, "Police might help tourists feel safe, But for locals, especially low-wage workers like street vendors and promoters, the police ensure that their movements are more tightly controlled. Remember those famous welcome parties, the ones that Dr. Medina started in the '80s? A lot of locals aren't invited. In fact, they're explicitly told to stay away."], [4, "There's a lot of expectations around where people are allowed to be on those days. Like, you know, definitely do not show up at the party unless you're helping to work there. And so on a day like the welcome party, that involves sort of restricting the movement of other folks who live there and making sure that, you know, they're only seeing and experiencing what the sort of town elites want them to see and experience to ensure that the reputation of the tourist industry is upheld."], [1, "Despite all this effort towards safety, whenever violence does occur anywhere in the border region, it can have major ripple effects on medical tourism. I spoke to someone named David Viquist, a medical tourism researcher who conducted a bunch of surveys on Americans after an incident in 2023 when 4 American medical tourists were ambushed by a drug cartel and only 2 of them survived. David was curious about Americans' perceived ideas about safety in Mexico and how those ideas might impact their decision to engage in medical tourism there. On one of his surveys, he asked Americans, \"Would you feel safe going to Mexico?\" And then another question: \"Would you feel safe going to Canc\u00fan?\" David's research suggested that Americans perceive Mexico and Canc\u00fan as two separate places. Canc\u00fan is safe. Mexico is not. In a way, I think Los Algodones has achieved that Canc\u00fan status. Molar City can exist as a performance for American consumers, disembodied from the forces that created it. And about how many people do you think in town have the same kind of job that you do?"], [3, "Oh, my competition."], [1, "Does it feel like competition?"], [3, "Well, I got them all, you know, I got them all pointed out. I got a report on every single one of them. Which one would you want me to start with? I got\u2014 my competition's everywhere. Everybody's my competition."], [1, "There are dozens of street promoters like Alberto, and because there are so many of them, all working on commission, Alberto's success depends on his charisma. He has to give the best performance. And so Alberto has his own strategies to try to stand out and make a good impression. Besides workshopping comedic material on the job, he told me this one other thing. He tends to play up how American he is. It's not a hard thing for him to do because like many people who grew up along the border, Alberto traveled back and forth a lot. He lived in Arizona but crossed the border weekly. His English is good. He describes himself as being \"from the border.\"."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["medical tourism", "dental care", "border towns", "mexico tourism", "tourist safety", "policing", "street vendors", "local workers", "tourism industry", "violence", "drug cartels", "cultural performance", "competition", "american visitors", "social control"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778192127.3260043, "duration_sec": 4.026834487915039, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Governance and\nIntergovernmental\nRelations\nMinutes\nTuesday, March 31, 2026, 10:30 AM\nChair: Amanda Sawyer, Vice-Chair: Paul Kashmann\nMembers: Kevin Flynn, Stacie Gilmore, Serena Gonzales-Gutierrez, Amanda Sandoval, Jamie Torres\nStaff: Anne Wallace\nCommittee not meeting. Consent agenda included.\nConsent Items\n26-0384 Approves the Mayor\u2019s appointment to the Denver Community Corrections\nBoard. Approves the Mayor\u2019s appointment of the Honorable Cecelia\nEspenoza to serve as a member of the Denver Community Corrections\nBoard for a term from 9-1-2025 through 8-30-2028 or until a successor is\nduly appointed, citywide.\nCouncil Resolution 26-0384 was approved by consent.\n26-0385 Approves the Mayor\u2019s appointment to the Denver Community Corrections\nBoard. Approves the Mayor\u2019s appointment of Michael Mahon to serve as a\nmember of the Denver Community Corrections Board for a term from\n9-1-2025 through 8-30-2028 or until a successor is duly appointed,\ncitywide.\nCouncil Resolution 26-0385 was approved b"]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as co